window.FLOCK_DOC_SEARCH = [{"title":"Start here","url":"index.html","text":"FLOCK documentation This is the starting point for humans and agents working on the robotic-herding game. Updated 14 October 2026. [Open the HTML documentation](html/index.html) for linked pages, search, diagrams, code examples and a page table of contents. Open that file directly after cloning; no application server is required. Read in this order Document Use it for ------ [Requirements](REQUIREMENTS.md) Product intent, implemented behavior, pending requirements and open decisions [Architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md) Module ownership, shared contracts and safe change boundaries [Gameplay](systems/GAMEPLAY.md) Input, simulation, companions, rewards, profile and upgrades [World](systems/WORLD.md) Procedural routes, barriers, footprints and navigation [Presentation](systems/PRESENTATION.md) Rendering, animation, nature, weather and audio [Development and release](DEVELOPMENT.md) Build, tests, documentation generation, QA evidence and publishing [Issue tracker](ISSUES.md) Prioritized work, acceptance criteria and agent handoffs [P0/P1 validation](validation/P0-P1.md) Source checks, release evidence, reproducible scenarios and remaining limits [Quality record](QUALITY.md) Historical reviews and the boundaries of their evidence [Documentation reviews](REVIEW.md) Blind content/HTML findings, corrections and verification limits Source of truth Runtime behavior comes from source and tests. The requirements describe intended behavior and identify gaps; they do not make requested features exist. The issue tracker records pending work. Historical quality scores apply only to the reviewed revision. HTML is generated from Markdown; edit the Markdown and run npm run docs:build. F-025 is Done: the canonical game is https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/; the nested legacy address redirects there. The published migration is the preceding game revision. F-001/F-002/F-005/F-009/F-010/F-016/F-018 are source-implemented and in Review, awaiting independent review and applicable rendered/device verification. F-014 is Blocked because no physical phone is available. All P2 work remains Open. See the validation runbook for separate source and publication milestones. Examples and diagrams Node examples are intended to run from the repository root with dependencies installed. Snippets labeled illustrative describe a contract rather than a supported public API. Diagram arrows show ownership, calls or transitions, not network services. This application has no backend. The HTML copy uses local assets and a bundled Mermaid renderer. Without JavaScript, documentation text and code remain readable; diagram source remains available. Source links in the HTML point back to files in the cloned repository. The repository HTML files are viewable locally; GitHub's file browser displays their source rather than serving them as a website."},{"title":"Requirements","url":"requirements.html","text":"Flock product requirements Snapshot: 14 October 2026; integrated P0/P1 source batch on flock_3D_chatgpt-work (source checkpoints and evidence in [validation](validation/P0-P1.md)). Audience: implementers, reviewers and players checking intended behavior. Public campaign levels are 1–100; source indexes are 0–99. This document separates the implemented baseline from requested changes. Implemented baseline means present in the inspected source, not proven on a phone. Pending request means recorded user intent whose acceptance criteria are still open. Proposed / unresolved means an implementation choice that must not be presented as approved. F-025 is Done; F-001/F-002/F-005/F-009/F-010/F-016/F-018 are source-implemented / Review; F-014 is Blocked pending a physical phone. All P2 items remain Open. [ISSUES.md](ISSUES.md) owns priority, acceptance criteria and execution records. [QUALITY.md](QUALITY.md) owns historical test/review evidence and its limits. This document does not implement or deploy anything. Product and approved engine Flock: Little Robot, Big Pastures is a cheerful, true-3D robotic herding game for portrait phones, playable with one thumb. The player steers Pip, a robotic sheepdog, behind a flock to guide it along countryside trails to a pen, fenced field or barn. Rewards buy skills and companions; companions support the player rather than delivering the flock unattended. Preserve the rounded, colorful, handmade countryside and visible robotic character details. The requested visual direction was bright, organic, clay/stop-motion countryside inspired by Aardman; characters and assets remain original. The original engine request was ThreeRust. Investigation found no publicly documented reusable runtime SDK suitable for this repository; the user approved Three.js as the fallback. The implemented stack is Three.js/WebGL, JavaScript modules and Vite, with procedural geometry/textures and generated audio. Do not describe this as ThreeRust, three-rs or threers. See [README.md](../README.md) and [package.json](../package.json). The current product is a standalone static development build with local browser saves. Accounts, online multiplayer and cloud saves are outside the implemented baseline. Local development and explicit build:dev distributions include isolated QA tools; normal production builds exclude them. Implemented baseline to preserve Play and state - Drag the meadow or movement control to steer; WASD/arrows support desktop. Tap Woof, double-tap the meadow or press Space to bark. Bark applies a short herding burst; the current controller uses a 650 ms burst and 2,100 ms cooldown. Preserve single-thumb access when adding interactions. - Pip's proximity pushes animals away; grouping behavior respects barriers. Physical bodies cannot cross fences, bridge rails, rivers outside decks, trees or the map perimeter. Pip's signal may cross low wooden fences/rails, but barns, water and trees shield it. Companion attendance, movement and delivery use clear ground paths. - Delivery requires Pip near the destination, an unheld animal fully inside the shared rotated rectangular enclosure footprint and clear ground attendance. Saved animals stop moving. First valid arrival starts a 30–60 second recovery countdown; all delivered ends early, otherwise timeout freezes a partial result. HUD and results show score / possible score. Pause, menus, workshop and QA editing do not advance simulation time or admissions. - Pause/menu/workshop suspend gameplay and audio. Workshop and results support replay/continuation. Restart meadow resets the current scenario; confirmed Restart Game clears campaign progress, bolts and purchases and starts level 1. Cancelling preserves progress. - Music begins after user interaction and shares a mute control with wildlife/weather ambience. Pending audio activation must not undo pause; delayed music scheduling must stay bounded. Code: [main.js](../src/main.js) (begin, bark, menu, confirmRestart, complete, frame), [simulation.js](../src/simulation.js) (stepSimulation), [audio.js](../src/audio.js). Regressions: UI, simulation, audio and herding-journey tests under [tests/](../tests/). This diagram summarizes current player states. Workshop return follows its originating gameplay state: opening it through a paused gameplay menu and closing it resumes play; an intro visit returns to intro, and results return to results. The controller also opens the workshop directly from play. Restart confirmation/cancellation is omitted here. The recovery countdown starts only on the first valid attended arrival; travel beforehand has no deadline. Campaign, map size and routes The recorded user baseline asks for a small sheep-to-pen tutorial, increasing flock size and journey distance, procedural fields/rivers/bridges/roads/gates, and progression points unlocking skills and autonomous helpers. Later direction preserves compact single-path levels 1–5, asks for 1–2 routes at levels 6–15 and 1–3 routes from level 16, expands vertical roaming bounds by 3–4× from level 6, and continues increasing the playable expanse. Drone starts at 10; the more expensive Buddy starts at 20; other skills appear across the first fifteen levels. These are intent constraints. The table below records current generator rules, not newly proposed balance targets. Exact starting count 5, +3 animals per level and cap 65 are implementation-derived, not additional user-specified numbers. The current late generator chooses two or three routes within the requested one-to-three range; it does not generate single-route late levels. Deterministic default seed is index 7919 + 42; custom seeds are supported by createLevel. Index is clamped to 0–99. Aspect Current implementation ------ Animal count min(5 + 3 index, 65): levels 1–5 have 5, 8, 11, 14, 17; level 21 onward has 65. Journey distance continues growing. Species Sheep initially; from level 4 every fourth animal (ID divisible by 4) is a cow. Compact levels 1–5 Canonical bounds: travel axis −32 to 30 + 3 index, cross axis −24 to +24. At level 1 this is 62 × 48 world units; at level 5, 74 × 48. Expanded levels 6–100 Canonical travel bounds −40 to 63 + 3 index; half cross extent 84 + 14 floor(index / 15). Level 6 is 118 × 168 before rotation/final bounds adjustment. The generator additionally expands final Z bounds to cover ±half cross extent. Destination distance Canonical destination axis 15 + 3 index for levels 1–5, 48 + 3 index thereafter; spawn is −20 early, −26 later. These are world-coordinate distances, not completion times. Route count Levels 1–5: one. Levels 6–15: one or two (zero-based odd indexes get two). Level 16 onward: two or three (indexes divisible by 3 get three). Direction First level fixed; other early levels use two opposing orientations; later levels rotate across four compass directions according to seed. Water/gates Level 1 has no river; other early levels one. Later levels one until level 15, two from level 16. Gate openings begin at level 3; operable closed gates are pending F-006. Destinations Repeat pen, field, barn by index modulo 3. Target radius is a sizing input 4.7 + 0.2 sqrt(animal count); shared rectangular half-extents are radius + 1.4, with a 7-unit entrance. Highlight, map, walls and full-body admission derive from the footprint. Roads and bridges Usable roads are 4.1 units early, except level 2 at 9.4, and 4.8 later; decorative shoulders are excluded. Deck width is exactly 1.25 × usable road width. Decks and straight road approaches align perpendicular to the sampled river segment. Length remains river width + 5 for dry landings. Examples: level 8 is index 7, has 26 robots and two routes; level 17 is index 16 and is the first level eligible for the requested, currently absent bolting behavior. The special wide level-2 crossing repaired a demonstrated simulated retrieval stall; preserve that regression when changing bridge rules. Maps provide alternate trails, winding roads, real bridge/gate openings, destination staging room, continuous rivers beyond boundaries and decorative hills outside playable ground. Alternate routes are generated by level rules, not purchased or independently unlocked. See [world.js](../src/world.js) (createLevel, canOccupy, terrainHeight, navigationTarget, openingSteer) and world/journey tests. Geometry-route reachability is not proof that every human can deliver every flock. Progress, economy and helpers The initial profile has zero bolts, no upgrades, no completed levels and only level 1 available. Any positive delivery at completion unlocks the next meadow (capped at level 100). Possible score is 80 + 25 index; earned score is floor(possibleScore saved / total). A run pays only max(0, score - previousLifetimeBestEarnedBolts). Equal/worse replays pay zero; later improvement pays only the delta even after spending/reloading. Zero deliveries neither pay nor unlock. Profile version 2 stores an authoritative bestEarnedBolts ledger; legacy completed levels migrate to fully earned entitlements without crediting spendable points again. Purchased tiers remain saved, but only tiers unlocked for the meadow being played apply. Invalid profile fields are sanitized; legacy boolean purchases migrate to tier 1. Browser storage failure retains the session and shows feedback. Key: flock-foundry-v1; do not clear or rename it for F-025. Skill Tier unlock levels Tier costs (bolts) Current tier effects ------------ Turbo paws 2 / 6 / 11 80 / 120 / 200 +1.8 / +2.7 / +3.5 m/s Friendly signal 7 / 10 / 15 120 / 180 / 260 +2 / +3 / +4 m range Scout drone 10 / 14 / 18 200 / 360 / 540 Support strength 0.7 / 0.93 / 1.17 Buddy dog 20 / 24 / 28 600 / 850 / 1200 Support strength 1 / 1.3 / 1.58 Happy bark 4 / 8 / 13 100 / 160 / 240 +0.25 / +0.45 / +0.65 strength Flock harmony 12 / 14 / 15 160 / 220 / 320 +12% / +23% / +35% regrouping Drone unlock is exactly level 10; Buddy is exactly 20 and costs more. Helpers attend the largest connected herd, hold it when Pip leaves, and do not fetch isolated stragglers or autonomously finish. Recover stragglers and return to lead delivery. Ground Buddy must navigate around solid barriers. See [progression.js](../src/progression.js), profile functions in [simulation.js](../src/simulation.js), progression/companion tests. Art, wildlife and weather Current sheep/cows have articulated, instanced stop-motion leg poses. Ground uses deterministic tileable color/bump textures, with decorative grass, flowers and reeds. Puffy model clouds drift with soft ground shadows. Butterflies, birds and simple squirrels avoid nearby robots, including saved flock animals and helpers. There are no rabbits or weather-driven shelter system yet. Small decorative bushes already mark field edges/tree roots; the broader requested F-019 bush environment remains Open. weatherForLevel in [nature.js](../src/nature.js) uses sun for levels 1–5, then cycles sun/wind/rain/sun/snow, disallowing snow through level 8. Non-sun intensity is currently 0.3; weather is fixed per level and does not affect movement or rewards. Current counts are five clouds, five birds, three squirrels, and eight butterflies (four in rain, zero in snow), subject to safe placement. These fixed counts do not satisfy F-017's random area-scaled populations. Animated rainfall is not accumulated wetness, storms or puddles. Requested change traceability Status: F-025 is Done; F-001/F-002/F-005/F-009/F-010/F-016/F-018 are Review (source implementation, pending independent/applicable rendered or device checks); F-014 is Blocked. Other rows remain Open. Existing systems must not be confused with completed visual acceptance. Detailed reproduction evidence, dependencies and decisions are in the corresponding sections of [ISSUES.md](ISSUES.md). ID Priority Required outcome and exact constraints Current gap / main code touchpoints ------------ F-025 P0, first Canonical public game at https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/; deploy dev2/ on gh-pages, preserve unrelated files and saved profiles; explicitly migrate old URL. Done: verified canonical migration at Pages baca95c7ba618d736730194b433254bf5058dd66; legacy redirect preserves query/fragment. New P1 source release is separate. F-001 P1 Continuous textured path corners without black triangles, gaps, spikes or flicker; check reported levels 4/8. Review: surfaces.js unions stroke pieces into disjoint upward triangles with world UVs. Pixel/flicker checks remain. F-002 P1 Bark above grass/path/ground and bridge decks, normally occluded by fences/rails. Review: barkSurface splits at deck/plank edges, lifts above each surface, retains depth testing and bounded pause-aware lifetime. F-003 P2 Detailed recognizable squirrel; coordinated scurry/bound/alert motion, stable idle/blocked heading, no spinning. Simple art and desired-direction heading exist. nature.js, models.js; actual motion review required. F-004 P2 Subtle animated running-water texture following local river direction, seamless banks/bends, pause-safe. Rivers exist; requested flow treatment pending. render.js, world.js. F-005 P1 Crossings perpendicular to local river tangent; bridge width 1.25 × crossing path width; minimal safe dry-ground extension. Review: local sampled tangent, straight approaches and 1.25 × usable path width; level-2 road/deck widen together. Visual checks remain. F-006 P2 Gates requiring player opening at level 20+; visible/collidable state and single-thumb interaction. Levels 1–19 retain accessible openings. Existing gates are openings, no open/close state. world.js, simulation.js, main.js, renderer. F-007 P2 Stone walls replace fences on selected levels with consistent collision and usable entrances. Fence baseline exists; wall variants pending. world.js, models.js, render.js. F-008 P2 Soft textured clouds, potentially overlapping blurred blobs, 10–25% semi-transparency, drifting/shadowed and readable. Model clouds currently use .5 material opacity. Opacity interpretation needs confirmation. nature.js. F-009 P1 First valid arrival starts 30–60 s countdown by complexity; finish at zero or all delivered; partial score shown as score / possible score. Review: first-arrival countdown, proportional floor score, positive partial unlock and best-improvement ledger implemented; verify human pacing. F-010 P1 Highlight, delivery region, minimap and rotated pen/field/barn enclosure share the same shape. Review: shared rotated footprint drives enclosure, highlight, minimap and full-body admission; rendered comparison remains. F-011 P2 Pip tail light normally blue, flashes red on bark then restores reliably. Dedicated tail feedback absent. models.js, render.js, shared bark event. F-012 P2 From level 8, up to 15% eligible animals have 33% chance to wander away while not actively herded. From level 17, up to 10% chance to bolt for 1–3 s. New cohort/probability behavior absent; ordinary tiny idle motion is not this feature. simulation.js, world.js. F-013 P2 Investigate level-5 pacing with humans, retrieval/chokepoint evidence and unchanged ordinary-input regressions. Simulated level-5 time spike known; no confirmed human difficulty verdict. World/journey balance. F-014 P1 Real-phone controls, portrait readability, gestures, modals, sound and restart verified with device/revision evidence. Blocked: physical phone unavailable; native gestures, touch, pixels and listening still required. F-015 P3 Measure mobile GPU and repeated transition stability on normal, large/split herds and weather scenes. Submission/resource checks exist; phone frame times unmeasured. Set device targets before verdict. F-016 P1 Under cover, fade roof and wall nearest camera when occluding Pip/flock; restore stably; retain collision. Review: full segmented barn roof/walls with camera-ray material fades, coverage hysteresis, pause and cleanup; pixels remain unverified. F-017 P2 Add detailed hopping rabbits; random rabbit/squirrel populations scale with usable level area. Rabbits absent, squirrels fixed count. nature.js, models.js; seeded bounded generation. F-018 P1 Dev-only QA menu: level 0–100, add/remove bolts, pause and reposition individual animals using touch/mouse. Available in deployed dev build; absent in release. Review: isolated dev-only menu; 0 = tutorial shortcut, 1–100 = public campaign. Paused coordinate edits, bounded override, currency, reset and JSON export; production exclusion tested. F-019 P2 Organic varied bushes without blocked routes/spawns or hidden critical play areas. Open: small decorative root/field bushes exist; requested broader organic varied environment and collision policy remain pending. F-020 P2 Visibly larger trees while maintaining proportion, actor visibility and route clearance. Existing trees retain current dimensions. Target scale unresolved. World/model/render bounds. F-021 P2 Richer tree/bush foliage and bark detail/textures, cheerful organic appearance with mobile budget. Current simplified tree baseline; bushes pending. models.js, render.js. F-022 P2 Light rain/wind retain wildlife; worsening severity makes smaller species hide first, then larger; plausible return. Fixed per-level counts lack retreat/shelter transitions. nature.js, shared weather state. F-023 P2 Worsening rain/storms increase cloud cover/change lighting; lightning flashes paired with thunder. No storm/transition/event contract. nature.js, render.js, audio.js. F-024 P2 Sustained heavier rain gradually darkens grass/dirt; storms form puddles and animal footstep splashes; rivers respond coherently. No accumulation/puddle/splash system. nature.js, render.js, world.js. F-026 P3 Pause-menu Quit returns to the landing page while preserving saved progress. Open; distinct from Restart Game. In-progress-meadow resume policy unresolved. F-027 P2 Wildlife may cross fences and leave/reenter the game area. Open; robotic animals/dog remain physically fenced. Wildlife obstacle/entry rules need definition with F-017. Decisions still open Resolve only the choices needed for an implementation; independent repairs can proceed. Record actual approvals in ISSUES.md. The following are not approved defaults: - F-006/F-007: gate action and reclosing; wall selection/height and whether walls block herding signals. - F-008/F-023: whether “10–25%” means opacity 0.10–0.25, how overlapping blobs compose, and any storm exception. - F-011: whether Buddy shares Pip's tail convention on a real Buddy bark event. - F-012: eligible-cohort integer rounding, random opportunity interval, wander distance/speed/cooldown, definition of active herding, and whether bolt chance is conditional on wandering. A 33% chance is not permission to roll every frame. For a five-animal flock, the treatment of 15% eligibility requires an explicit rounding policy. - F-017/F-019/F-020: wildlife area/count ranges, caps, absence/spawn rules; bush collision policy; tree scale/range. - F-022–F-024: shared severity model, species thresholds/hysteresis/return delay, transition timing and within-level weather policy; lightning frequency/intensity/thunder timing; wetness/drying rates, puddle thresholds/placement, river response and which actors splash. No weather movement penalty is approved. This is a required coordination boundary, not a claim that a shared severity API has already been designed or approved. Implemented P1 decisions and deployment boundary User-approved on 14 October 2026: positive partial delivery unlocks the next meadow; proportional earned score uses floor(reward saved / total); lifetime best improvement pays only the delta; QA 0 is a tutorial shortcut and public levels remain 1–100. Implemented recovery duration is clamp(30 + ceil(max(0,total-5)/5) + 4(routes-1) + 2floor(spawnToGoalDistance/40), 30, 60) seconds of simulation time. This formula is a source balance choice to review, not measured human pacing. F-025 is Done. Canonical URL: https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/. Pages commit baca95c7ba618d736730194b433254bf5058dd66 deployed the preceding game at dev2/, with a query/fragment-preserving legacy redirect, matching hashes for all 15 game files and redirect, and unchanged 17 unrelated blobs. Existing local-storage key remains flock-foundry-v1; real browser continuity is still a device check. The helper validates the build's relative asset graph and stages canonical output without committing/pushing. Normal build excludes QA; build:dev explicitly includes it. Source completion, bundle checks, Pages commit, native publication and live verification are separate milestones. The current verified migration is not evidence that this P1 source batch is live. See [runbook](validation/P0-P1.md). Verification and handoff contract For each implemented request, record requirement ID, source commit, level/seed, expected/observed outcome, relevant commands/results and remaining limitations in ISSUES.md. Run meaningful regression checks and production build. Retain the solved companion hold/no-autonomous-delivery, level-10/20 unlock, level-2 bridge retrieval, profile/restart and audio pause/scheduling cases. npm test covers source simulation/profile/controller behavior, real Three.js geometry/matrices behind a substituted GPU interface, resource disposal and synthetic audio lifecycles. npm run build checks production packaging. Existing historical evidence reports 93 passing tests, route sweeps, bounded legitimate-input flock deliveries and fresh blind reviews at 9.1/10 technical / 8.6/10 gameplay. These are bounded historical findings from QUALITY.md, not a new assessment of this document or the pending requests. Headless checks cannot verify rendered pixels/flicker, motion quality, real Safari gestures, portrait touch ergonomics, perceived sound balance or phone GPU frame time. Screenshots establish visible artifacts; they do not establish temporal flicker/spinning or a root cause. Real-device records must identify device/browser and source/deployment revision, with scenario evidence. Preserve F-013/F-015 as Open and F-014 as Blocked until their specific evidence exists. Do not convert historical critic scores into a verified overall game score. Next: review the P1 source batch, complete rendered/device scenarios when available, and publish/verify that batch separately. Gates/walls/wandering and all other P2 requests remain Open. Coordinate shared files and the weather contract; do not treat QA tooling as a mandatory runtime dependency of fixes. This requirements snapshot has been checked against repository source and records only; it adds no gameplay changes or new browser/device validation."},{"title":"Architecture","url":"architecture.html","text":"Architecture and system contracts [Documentation home](README.md) · [Requirements](REQUIREMENTS.md) · [Issue tracker](ISSUES.md) The current game is a static browser application: Vite bundles JavaScript, CSS and local fonts; Three.js renders WebGL 2; WebAudio synthesizes music and ambience. Browser storage holds a validated profile. There is no server, account system, multiplayer or cloud save. Module map Source Responsibility Important boundary --------- src/main.js Browser UI state, input, game loop, profile actions, effect/audio dispatch Owns mode and pause; simulation is called only while playing src/world.js Seeded levels, routes, barrier queries, body movement and navigation Shared physical geometry; render geometry must agree src/simulation.js Herd movement, companion behavior, delivery and profile functions Mutates the level/dog; returns events, not DOM operations src/progression.js Upgrade catalog, gates, costs, validation and effects Ownership is separate from activation in the selected level src/models.js Robot/scenery model construction and shared model resources Shared material changes may affect many objects src/goal-view.js Shared-footprint highlight, border, barn floor and minimap polygon Consumes world corners; does not define admission src/surfaces.js Non-overlapping ribbon union and surface-aware bark geometry Geometry construction only; retains normal depth testing src/cutaway.js Camera-ray structure visibility and material clone lifecycle Changes visibility only, never collision or rewards src/qa.js Development-only isolated QA controls and scenario export Dynamically imported only in development/QA builds src/render.js Camera, scene, static/actor batches, map, transient effects Visual state must not decide rewards or collision src/nature.js Decorative weather, clouds and wildlife Current weather does not change gameplay rewards or control src/audio.js Music scheduling, UI/game sounds and ambience lifecycle Browser activation is asynchronous and can be invalidated Source links: [controller](../src/main.js), [world](../src/world.js), [simulation](../src/simulation.js), [progression](../src/progression.js), [renderer](../src/render.js), [models](../src/models.js), [nature](../src/nature.js), [audio](../src/audio.js). Data and coordinate contracts Public campaign levels are 1–100, while level.index and saved completion entries are 0–99. The QA selector maps 0 to the tutorial (index 0), and 1–100 to indexes 0–99. Do not equate index 100 with public level 100. The ground plane uses x and z; Three.js y is elevation. Some input APIs accept y as a compatibility alias for planar z; that does not change world coordinates. Headings are radians. Bridge longitudinal/lateral coordinates derive from the heading, not an axis-aligned box. The main loop requests animation frames and caps the per-frame simulation dt at 0.05 seconds. It is a variable-step loop, not an accumulator-based fixed-tick loop. Long frame gaps do not simulate the entire elapsed wall time. Headless fixtures commonly use fixed 1/60 increments for repeatability. js // Runnable: node --input-type=module from the repository root. import { createLevel, stepSimulation } from './src/simulation.js'; const level = createLevel(0); const dog = { ...level.dog }; const result = stepSimulation(level, dog, { x: 0, z: 0 }, 1 / 60, {}); console.log({ publicLevel: level.index + 1, saved: result.saved, total: result.total }); State ownership level contains mutable runtime animals, helper state, time and completion. dog is updated by the simulation. A version-2 profile contains points, paid upgrade tiers, positive-delivery completion indexes, highest unlocked index, music preference, and per-level bestEarnedBolts/bestScores ledgers. Creating/replaying a level replaces transient state; a completed run pays only its lifetime best-score improvement. bestEarnedBolts is authoritative across spending and reloading; bestScores and completion indexes are rebuilt from it when loading. Full Restart Game replaces profile progress with defaults, keeps music preference, persists the reset and begins the tutorial. Rendering consumes the same level and dog, adding mesh/cache/resource state of its own. Collision belongs in world/simulation queries. Minimap, target highlight, enclosure and full-body admission use targetFootprint, targetWorldPoint, targetLocalPoint and containsTarget from world.js. The target radius remains a sizing/attendance input, not a circular admission test. Cross-system changes Change Coordinate before implementation ------ Bridges/gates/walls Generator, physical footprint, navigation, render model, minimap and journey checks Goal shape/countdown/scoring Admission geometry, completion event, reward ledger, UI, replay policy and save migration Changing weather One severity/time contract shared by wildlife, clouds/light, audio and accumulated wetness Model size/detail Placement clearance, culling bounds, shared materials, shadows and disposal QA animal editing Pause ownership, selection/placement override, profile isolation and release gating F-009 and F-018 now have source implementations documented in Gameplay and the validation runbook. F-012/F-022–F-024 retain unresolved future design semantics. See [issue acceptance criteria](ISSUES.md). Resource and async invariants Level replacement must dispose owned geometries/materials/nature resources without destroying shared cached robot geometry. Culling must retain visible limbs and shadows as well as bodies. Temporary bark/celebration effects must finish and release resources. Audio start/resume may complete after a pause, newer activation or disposal. Generation guards invalidate stale requests. Never follow an awaited start with an unconditional resume. Music catch-up skips overdue steps arithmetically and bounds scheduling work rather than replaying a backlog. Evidence and extension points Tests run actual simulation/profile code and construct real Three.js geometry, with browser graphics/audio surfaces substituted where needed. They do not establish rendered appearance, physical Safari interactions, sound quality or mobile FPS. [Quality record](QUALITY.md) preserves those limits and historical review scores. Use [system documentation](README.md) for narrower APIs. Start work from [the tracker](ISSUES.md), claim ownership, retain regression checks and record source versus deployment milestones separately."},{"title":"Gameplay & progression","url":"gameplay.html","text":"Gameplay and progression This describes current source behavior, not the queued design in the [issue tracker](../ISSUES.md). Geometry and navigation contracts are in [WORLD.md](WORLD.md). Entry points: [simulation.js](../../src/simulation.js), [progression.js](../../src/progression.js), and browser orchestration in [main.js](../../src/main.js). State and interfaces The simulation is renderer-independent ES modules, directly importable in Node. stepSimulation(level, dog, input = {}, dt = 1 / 60, upgrades = {}) mutates the level, animals, and supplied dog; it also assigns level.dog = dog. The browser initially copies the generated dog so callers should use that assignment rather than expect the original object identity to persist. Value Contract ------ level.index Zero-based campaign index, 0–99. UI level number is index + 1. Positions/input Ground plane {x, z} in world units; input y is accepted only as a fallback for missing z. Renderer height is separate. Heading Radians, forward vector {x: sin(heading), z: cos(heading)}. Animal {id, type: 'sheep' \\ 'cow', x, z, vx, vz, saved}; stepping adds heading, alert and optional pressure timestamp. Input {x, z, bark}; direction normalized and magnitude capped at 1, so diagonals do not run faster. Result {saved, total, score, possibleScore, completed, justCompleted, reward, remaining, countdownStarted, events, helpers, dogCollision}. Events {type:'animalSaved', id, animalType} {type:'countdownStarted', duration} on first valid arrival, and one {type:'levelCompleted', index, reward, reason} per fresh level instance. stepSimulation defaults to 1/60 second and clamps supplied dt to [0, 0.1]. This is not a fixed-step browser loop. main.js calls once per animation frame using elapsed frame time capped at 0.05 seconds, with no accumulator or catch-up. Tests often choose fixed 1/60 or 0.1 steps. level.time and browser completion elapsed advance only during play, using these capped deltas; elapsed is not full wall-clock time after a long stall. Bark lasts 650 ms with a 2100 ms cooldown measured by performance.now(), independently of simulation time. Pip moves at 7.8 + speedBoost units/second, with radius 0.62. Movement collision reports blockedX/blockedZ; see the [world clearance distinction](WORLD.mdclearance-is-not-one-query). Sheep radius/speed limit are 0.55/4.2, cows 0.78/3.3. These are kinematic bodies, not a rigid-body engine. One simulation step Each active animal computes forces from a snapshot, so earlier animals' updated positions do not directly affect later ones' force inputs. Separation acts under 2 units; cohesion/alignment use visible neighbors under 18 units. Ground visibility is cached by animal pair and 0.2-unit position cells. Cohesion starts beyond 2.6 units from neighbor center; alignment retains 45% of average neighbor velocity. Unpressured animals also receive small deterministic sinusoidal wandering. Saved animals leave the active flock. Pip's base pressure radius is 7.6 + signalBoost, plus 3.3 while barking. Within it, a clear player signal produces flee pressure; bark multiplies urgency by 1.65 + barkBoost. Animals flee Pip's position, not the destination. Under pressure, openingSteer can nudge sideways toward a nearby crossing/gate when forward travel is blocked. Velocity smoothing, speed caps, solid body movement, and limited angled escape attempts then resolve movement. Companion support and admission use ground paths; do not replace those with the more permissive player signal. Companions support the player Owned companion tiers apply only after their current meadow's unlock gates. The largest connected component is selected using links of at most 6.5 units with a clear ground path (radius 0.15). Ties prefer members from the previous selection, then lowest animal ID. Recompute on active-membership changes or after more than 0.25 simulation seconds. A singleton is not a supported herd: helpers go to standby, and isolated stragglers receive no support. Attendance uses the closest ground-visible member of the selected herd, not its centroid. Visibility search is limited to 18 units, so losing a clear path yields infinite attendance distance even if Pip is physically close. Existing mode Hold decision ------ Initial/standby Hold when nearest visible member is farther than 14. Hold Stay holding at distance 12 or greater; return to assist below 12. Assist Hold beyond 18; stay assisting at 18 or below. Hold entry captures the herd centroid and each member's position. Membership change with less than half overlap also recaptures positions. Holding suppresses inherited alignment/cohesion momentum and steers members back toward stored positions. Direct pressure of at least 0.03 overrides holding for that animal. It is a bounded positional behavior, not an immutable freeze. In assist, companions tidy lateral flanks relative to Pip→centroid. The force vectors have their mean removed, preventing net propulsion toward a destination. The drone flies over the centroid; Buddy navigates solid ground alongside Pip, or alongside the herd while holding. Buddy's body has no blanket flee signal. Neither companion chooses a goal or autonomously delivers the flock. Tests: [companions.test.js](../../tests/companions.test.js) covers selection, hysteresis, fifteen-minute hold, straggler retrieval, blocked signals, zero goal dependence and unattended admission. [herding-journey.test.js](../../tests/herding-journey.test.js) uses legitimate dog input to complete five representative crossing journeys; its bounded policy does not prove all seeds/routes solvable. Admission, rewards, and profile lifecycle Admission requires positive simulation time, Pip within target.radius + 12 of target center, an animal not held by companions, its entire circular body inside the shared rectangular target footprint, and Pip→animal clear ground path (radius .15). containsTarget contracts both local half-extents by the physical body radius; walls remain physical barriers. Saved animals stop moving. The first valid arrival emits countdownStarted and sets level.delivery = {started:true,duration,remaining}. There is no travel deadline before it. Duration is clamp(30 + ceil(max(0,total-5)/5) + 4(max(1,routeCount)-1) + 2floor(spawnToGoalDistance/40),30,60) seconds. The first-arrival step receives the full duration; later positive steps subtract capped simulation dt before movement. When time reaches zero admission closes before movement, freezing level.result = {saved,total,score,possibleScore,reason:'timeout'}. All arrivals finish immediately with reason all-arrived. Completed levels do not step again. Empty flocks do not complete. Score is floor(level.reward saved / total), with possible score 80 + 25index. A simulation result's one-shot reward is the run score; it is not the actual wallet delta. applyLevelReward validates the profile and pays max(0,score - bestEarnedBolts[index]), updates the lifetime ledger, and unlocks the next meadow on any positive delivery. Equal/worse replays and repeated application pay zero. Spending points does not reduce lifetime entitlement. Zero saved gives no bolts/unlock. Index 99 remains the last unlocked index; the browser next button cycles to the first meadow after a positive result there. defaultProfile() returns {version:2, points:0, upgrades:{}, completedLevels:[], bestScores:{}, bestEarnedBolts:{}, highestLevel:0, music:true}. validateProfile bounds points to whole numbers 0–1e9 and validates owned tiers. bestEarnedBolts is canonical: valid numeric positive ledger entries use canonical index keys 0–99 and are floored/capped at each level's maximum reward. It rebuilds bestScores and sorted completed indexes from that ledger; stale caches cannot inflate entitlements. Highest unlocked index is reconciled and capped at 99; only explicit music:false disables music. Pre-ledger version 1/unversioned completed levels migrate to full earned entitlement without adding spendable points or changing ownership. Version-2 completion caches cannot trigger that migration. Profile saves contain no in-progress world positions. loadProfile(storage?, key = 'flock-foundry-v1') and saveProfile(profile, storage?, key?) use a storage object with getItem/setItem (default localStorage). Invalid JSON or inaccessible storage loads defaults; saving failure returns false. The browser retains its in-memory profile and displays a toast on save failure. The same key survives normal restarts and deployment path changes on the same origin. begin(index) regenerates a meadow, resets elapsed/input/tips/bark state, and keeps profile progress. “Restart this meadow” calls it with the current index. Confirmed “Restart Game” replaces the profile with defaults while retaining music, persists it, resets reward/tap state, and begins index 0. Cancel does not reset progress. Pause/workshop modes stop stepping; the renderer can still update visual effects in intro/completion. Upgrade contract The six three-tier catalog entries and exact costs/gates live in [progression.js](../../src/progression.js). Use its exports rather than duplicate a pricing table in callers: Export Purpose ------ upgradeLevel, validateUpgrades Accept positive integer tiers (cap at 3); migrate legacy true to tier 1; drop unknown/malformed entries. upgradeCost, nextUnlockLevel Price/gate of next tier; null for unknown or maxed upgrade. upgradeAvailability Workshop status with unknown, owned, locked, points, or null reason. purchaseUpgrade Atomically purchase one tier; failed purchase preserves points. activeUpgrades, upgradeEffects Clamp owned tiers to gates of the current one-based meadow number. Purchase eligibility follows unlocked campaign progress (highestLevel and completions). Activation follows the meadow being played. Thus an owned later tier stays in the save when replaying an early meadow but its effect is inactive there. Effects are absolute arrays by tier, not cumulative additions of each previous tier. See [progression tests](../../tests/progression.test.js) and [simulation tests](../../tests/simulation.test.js). Run from repository root with Node 22+; no DOM or WebGL is needed: sh node --input-type=module \u003c\u003c'JS' import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; import { createLevel, stepSimulation, defaultProfile, applyLevelReward, purchaseUpgrade, saveProfile, loadProfile, } from './src/simulation.js'; import { activeUpgrades } from './src/progression.js'; const level = createLevel(0); // Synthetic admission fixture, as opposed to a player input policy. level.animals.forEach(a => Object.assign(a, {x:level.target.x, z:level.target.z})); const result = stepSimulation(level, {...level.target}, {}, 1/60); assert.equal(result.justCompleted, true); const profile = defaultProfile(); assert.equal(applyLevelReward(profile, level), 80); assert.equal(applyLevelReward(profile, level), 0); assert.equal(purchaseUpgrade(profile, 'speed').ok, true); assert.deepEqual(activeUpgrades(profile.upgrades, 1), {}); assert.deepEqual(activeUpgrades(profile.upgrades, 2), {speed:1}); let json; const storage = {getItem: () => json, setItem: (_key, value) => {json=value;}}; assert.equal(saveProfile(profile, storage), true); assert.deepEqual(loadProfile(storage), profile); console.log('completion, reward, tier gates and storage round trip passed'); JS Development QA and remaining work qa.js is dynamically imported only when Vite defines __FLOCK_QA__ for development or explicit qa mode. Production builds eliminate the entry point and mutation module. First QA entry replaces the in-memory profile with defaults, begins the tutorial and marks an isolated session; persistence is suppressed until reload. Opening the dialog pauses input, gameplay, countdowns and audio and makes background controls inert. Resume plays an active edited test world; for a completed meadow it restores results without re-awarding bolts. Reset starts a fresh test profile/tutorial. Reload returns to the normal saved profile. QA 0 maps to tutorial index 0; QA 1–100 map to public levels/indexes 0–99 without unlock restrictions. Bolt edits accept nonnegative safe integers, reject overflow and clamp removals at zero. Animal coordinate edits require paused QA mode, finite coordinates inside the meadow bounds and body-clear occupancy, unless explicit invalid-placement override bypasses occupancy. They zero velocity and preserve delivered state. A select identifies species/ID and delivery state; coordinate inputs support mouse/touch forms. Export downloads JSON containing seed, level, elapsed time, profile, dog and animal/world state; import is not provided. No window mutation API is installed. The [tracker](../ISSUES.md) retains all P2 work Open, including gates, wanderers and pacing. P1 source implementation is in Review; headless checks do not establish real-phone feel, rendered visuals or listening quality. See [runbook](../validation/P0-P1.md) for release and manual checks."},{"title":"World & navigation","url":"world.html","text":"World generation, collision, and navigation Current contracts come from [world.js](../../src/world.js); [GAMEPLAY.md](GAMEPLAY.md) explains its simulation callers. The [issue tracker](../ISSUES.md) is the authority for proposed geometry and gameplay changes. This module imports neither renderer nor browser APIs. Coordinates and generated state Positions use {x, z} on a flat ground plane; vertical height belongs to rendering. Distances are Euclidean world units. Heading is radians, with forward {x:sin(h), z:cos(h)} and lateral {x:cos(h), z:-sin(h)}. Do not assume destination progress always means increasing world X. createLevel(index = 0, seed = index 7919 + 42) floors/clamps the index to 0–99 and converts the seed to unsigned 32-bit. The default parameter expression uses the argument before index normalization; pass explicit valid indexes for ordinary campaign reproducibility. The same index/seed yields deeply equal fresh state. Randomness is a private seeded generator, not Math.random(). Field Meaning ------ index, seed, title, description, reward Campaign metadata; displayed level is index + 1; reward is 80 + index 25. bounds {minX,maxX,minZ,maxZ} world-space axis-aligned playable bounds. spawn, dog, animals Flock origin, Pip origin, initial body states. target {x,z,radius,type,heading}; type cycles pen → field → barn by index modulo 3. rivers Sampled centerline points, width, rotated bridge deck descriptors. fenceSegments Physical {a,b,kind} segments: boundary, interior, target. fences currently starts empty. gates Open gap descriptors, not animated door state. routes, roads Sampled routes {id,points,length}; roads reference route points plus width. obstacles, sceneryHills Circular tree collision footprints; decorative hills outside bounds. Simulation attaches runtime state such as time, completion, helpers and visibility caches. Do not reuse a stepped level as a pristine generation fixture. Generation starts in canonical longitudinal/lateral coordinates, then rotates by quarter turns. Early indexes 0–4 have a 48-unit lateral footprint, one route and longitudinal end 15 + 3index. Index 0 points east; other early levels choose east/west from seed parity. Later levels choose all four directions by seed % 4, use longitudinal end 48 + 3index, and lateral half-width 84 + floor(index/15)14; transformed bounds also retain at least that world-Z span. Thus rotated late bounds may be larger than simply rotating the canonical rectangle. Flock count is min(5 + 3index, 65). From index 3, every fourth animal is a cow. Bodies start in a compact grid with seeded jitter and Pip behind them. If a curved bank clips a body, the generator tries up to seven half-unit backward translations of the entire grid, preserving its shape. This finite correction is tested on selected custom seeds; it is not a formal proof for every unsigned seed. Generation pipeline and routes Index 0 has no river. Indexes 1–4 have one; indexes 5–14 have one; indexes 15+ have two. River centerlines are gentle sinusoids sampled every 2 canonical lateral units, extending beyond the fenced map. Each route gets a bridge over each river. Route counts are one for early levels; thereafter one on even indexes/two on odd indexes below 15; indexes 15+ have three when divisible by 3, otherwise two. From index 2, an interior fence has open gate gaps for each route. Routes connect spawn, bridge approaches/centers, gates and destination approaches. wander samples each leg roughly every 1.2 units; ordinary legs gain seeded meanders. Legs adjoining bridges/gates or ending at target stay straight. A candidate meander is kept only if every sampled segment has 0.85-unit ground clearance against already-built geometry; otherwise that leg uses straight interpolation. Trees are added afterward, excluded from a 7 + tree.radius corridor around route segments, within 14 of spawn, or within target.radius + 10 of destination. Maximum attempted tree count is min(10 + 2index,130), so actual counts vary. Roads are visual/guidance data, not forces. Nothing in stepSimulation attracts animals toward routes or target. The route API can guide Pip and ground companions; player pressure drives delivery. Shared bridge and enclosure geometry A bridge {x,z,width,length,heading} is a straight rotated rectangle. In its local frame a body is over the deck when longitudinal distance is at most length/2 + radius and lateral distance at most width/2 - radius. The longitudinal extension lets bodies enter from dry ground; lateral contraction preserves body clearance. Bridge length is river width plus 5. Deck heading is perpendicular to the actual sampled river segment at its center; the bridge approach, center and departure are collinear. Usable path widths are 9.4 at index 1, 4.1 at other early indexes and 4.8 later; deck width is exactly 1.25 × that width. Decorative shoulders are excluded. The wide level-2 crossing is preserved by widening its road together with its deck. Later branches turn on common dry planes to avoid cutting neighboring rail ends. Each side rail runs from -length/2 - 0.1 to length/2 + 0.1 at lateral ±width/2, with beam radius 0.05 and four post footprints of radius 0.065 per side. Rails remain solid beyond the wet river edge, so a body on dry land beside a deck cannot cut sideways through its end rail. It must walk around an open end. Rail and river bounds are cached in WeakMaps keyed by geometry object; treat generated descriptors as static or replace the object when editing rather than mutating cached geometry in place. terrainHeight(level,x,z) returns a deck arch 0.18 + 0.12sin(π(along/length + 0.5)) inside the zero-radius bridge footprint, otherwise 0. It does not change collision into a 3D physics system. See [render.js](../../src/render.js) for presentation of these descriptors. targetFootprint(target) defines a rotated rectangle with half-length/half-width target.radius + 1.4, a 7-unit entrance on the upstream side, world corners and entrance point. targetLocalPoint converts positions to forward/lateral coordinates; targetWorldPoint performs the inverse. containsTarget(target,point,bodyRadius) requires the entire circular body within both edges. createLevel derives the five target wall segments from that same footprint. Thus corner regions outside the former circle are admissible when the full body is inside; a center sitting on a wall is insufficient. The renderer's goal-view.js uses these world corners for the highlight surface, outline and barn floor, and projects them for the minimap. Square enclosure rotation is expressed in world vertices, avoiding inconsistent plane rotation conventions. Target radius still sets size and Pip attendance distance; it no longer defines admission. See [admission](GAMEPLAY.mdadmission-rewards-and-profile-lifecycle) and geometry/goal-view tests. Clearance is not one query Export What it answers ------ canOccupy(level,x,z,radius=.55) May a circular body occupy one point? Bounds inset by radius + .18, fence thickness .18, expanded trees/water and solid bridge rails all apply. Water is traversable only within a bridge footprint. moveBody(level,body,dx,dz,radius=.55) Mutates position with steps of at most .25 units, tries combined displacement, then axis sliding; returns {blockedX,blockedZ}. It does not set velocity or heading. hasClearGroundPath(level,a,b,radius=.55) Is a straight ground segment clear? Solid fences/rails, trees, water and inset endpoint bounds apply. Water checks sample at intervals no greater than .45 units. hasClearPlayerSignal(level,a,b) Radius .15 audible pressure line: ignores low fences and bridge rails; target fence segments shield only barns. Trees, water outside decks and bounds still block. separatedByBank(level,a,b) Specialized river/rail separation query, not general obstacle clearance or a substitute for ground path validation. The player signal is used for Pip's pressure only. Flock cohesion/alignment visibility, companion selection/attendance/support and destination attendance use solid ground paths. A sheep can hear Pip over a low pen rail while neither body can cross it; that does not mean a companion may pull through it or admission may ignore it. Physical separation forces between very close animals are distinct from neighbor visibility. moveBody subdivides displacement to limit tunneling; stepSimulation additionally caps dt. Neither is a swept 3D rigid-body solver. Rivers are sampled polylines with radius-expanded checks, and trees are circular footprints, not mesh intersection tests. Navigation exports Export Behavior and limit ------ distance, closestOnSegment, segmentDistance Shared ground-plane geometry; projection result includes clamped t. routeProgress(level,position,routeId?) Nearest projection on matching sampled route segments; returns route/id, segment index, point, off-route distance, arclength progress and route length; null if none. nextNavigationPoint(level,position,routeId?) First sampled point at least 7 arclength units after projection, otherwise target. This is not exactly a point seven straight-line units away. navigationTarget(level,body,desired,radius=.62) Returns desired when clear, otherwise route projection/lookahead; near bridge rails builds a small visibility graph around up to two bridges to walk around open ends. It is a local waypoint heuristic, not global pathfinding for arbitrary user geometry. destinationApproach(level,body) Oriented waypoint around enclosure walls toward upstream entrance. Used by journey-test player policy, not an animal's automatic goal drive. openingSteer(level,body,vx,vz) Adds lateral steering toward the closest opening on a nearby pressed barrier. Only used under player pressure; alias steeringOpening is also exported. Run this from repository root with Node 22+: sh node --input-type=module \u003c\u003c'JS' import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; import { createLevel, distance, canOccupy, moveBody, hasClearGroundPath, routeProgress, nextNavigationPoint, terrainHeight, } from './src/world.js'; const level = createLevel(23, 201); assert.deepEqual(level, createLevel(23, 201)); const bridge = level.rivers[0].bridges[0]; assert.equal(canOccupy(level, bridge.x, bridge.z, .8), true); assert.ok(Math.abs(terrainHeight(level, bridge.x, bridge.z) - .3) \u003c 1e-8); const route = level.routes[0], walker = {...level.spawn}; for (const point of route.points) { assert.ok(hasClearGroundPath(level, walker, point, .8)); moveBody(level, walker, point.x-walker.x, point.z-walker.z, .8); assert.ok(distance(walker, point) \u003c .001); } assert.ok(distance(walker, level.target) \u003c .001); const info = routeProgress(level, level.spawn, route.id); assert.equal(info.routeId, route.id); assert.ok(nextNavigationPoint(level, level.spawn, route.id)); console.log('deterministic world, deck height and traversable route passed'); JS Evidence and queued changes [world.test.js](../../tests/world.test.js) checks all 100 default campaign indexes for cow-clear route traversal with actual moveBody calls, safe default spawns, destination margins, exterior hills and geometry constraints. It also covers selected custom seeds, four rotations, audible-versus-solid fences and dry-end rail navigation. [simulation.test.js](../../tests/simulation.test.js) checks actual movement against banks, bounds and destination walls. [herding-journey.test.js](../../tests/herding-journey.test.js) covers five bounded full-delivery journeys. Those scopes are narrower than all possible seeds or real-device verification. Bridge alignment/width and shared enclosure geometry are source-implemented / Review; interactive gates, stone walls and broader environment changes remain Open in [ISSUES.md](../ISSUES.md). F-025 deployment migration is Done; the P1 batch awaits a separate publication. Geometry evidence is not rendered/device acceptance."},{"title":"Rendering, nature & audio","url":"presentation.html","text":"Presentation: renderer, nature and audio This describes the implementation at the documentation baseline, not a promise that the open art requests are complete. The application uses Three.js 0.180.0 and generated geometry/textures; there is no ThreeRust runtime, downloaded model pipeline or recorded soundtrack. Source behavior and headless tests are inspectable; real browser rendering, phone performance and perceived sound balance remain unverified here. See [quality scope](../QUALITY.md) and the [open issue tracker](../ISSUES.md). Boundaries and frame data flow [main.js](../../src/main.js) owns the animation loop, application mode and the FarmAudio instance. Simulation owns positions and gameplay decisions. [render.js](../../src/render.js) reads the level and separate player-dog state to build scene transforms, then renders; [nature.js](../../src/nature.js) maintains its own decorative wildlife/cloud state. Wildlife never pushes the herd or awards progress. The frame interval is capped at .05 seconds. Simulation and event sounds run only in playing. The renderer receives positive dt in playing, intro and complete; other modes pass zero. Ambience updates only in playing. Thus completion/intro can animate visual scenery without advancing gameplay; a modal freezes renderer time but the frame loop still redraws. Backgrounding during play opens the menu, which pauses audio and resets input. There is no main-loop call to FarmAudio.dispose() on page teardown. The integration has this shape (abridged from main.js; mode handling is essential): js const dt = Math.min((now - last) / 1000, .05); if (mode === 'playing') { const result = stepSimulation(level, dog, input, dt, profile.upgrades); for (const event of result.events) if (event.type === 'animalSaved') audio.play('saved'); } renderer.update(level, dog, ['playing', 'intro', 'complete'].includes(mode) ? dt : 0, mode === 'intro'); if (mode === 'playing') audio.updateAmbience(dt, { weather: level.weather renderer.nature?.weather?.type 'sun' }); Interface Consumer / responsibility --- --- new MeadowRenderer(canvas) Creates WebGL renderer, camera, shared materials, scene groups and nature root. Requires browser Canvas/WebGL globals. setLevel(level) Replaces level-owned presentation resources; builds terrain, structures, scenery and species batches. update(level, dog, dt, preview=false) Camera follow, poses, nature, particles, per-instance culling, final draw. Animal array order must match the last setLevel. resize() Reads viewport size and aspect; projection changes on the next update. bark() / celebrate(x,z) Presentation effects triggered by application actions/events. drawMap(canvas, level, dog) Separate Canvas 2D map derived from world coordinates, not a scene render. Main updates it at roughly 10 Hz. MeadowNature.setLevel/update/dispose Owns cosmetic weather/fauna state and all its per-level GPU resources. FarmAudio.start/resume/pause/setMuted/play/updateAmbience/dispose Owns Web Audio lifecycle, music timer, procedural sources and cleanup. Scene construction and materials MeadowRenderer configures antialiasing, pixel ratio capped at 1.6, sRGB output, ACES filmic tone mapping and exposure 1.05. A hemisphere light and moving directional sun light the scene. The sun has a 1024-square PCF soft shadow map and an orthographic shadow volume of ±30 units. Background and fog share a pale blue color; fog spans 95–195 world units. These are configuration values, not measured quality/performance guarantees. setLevel builds a plane larger than the bounds, decorative hills, road/river ribbons, bridge meshes, target marker, fences, structures, obstacle trees and bounded decorative details. Roads and rivers use the sampled centerlines supplied by the world. Road shoulders may wander cosmetically; river width follows the physical half-width. Fence/gate/target segments come from world collision geometry. surfaces.js builds ribbons by unioning convex stroke pieces into disjoint trapezoids, then upward triangles with world-coordinate UVs. Bevel center fans bound sharp joins. The union removes stacked coplanar pieces at turns, reversals and self-crossings. Headless topology checks support the repair; rendered flicker acceptance remains pending. [models.js](../../src/models.js) supplies Pip, flock species, helpers, vegetation and structures. Its primitive geometry cache is keyed by constructor/arguments, and its material cache persists across levels. bakeModel clones transformed component geometry, merges it by material, disposes temporary clones and returns fewer meshes. Model clones share the baked geometry/materials. createAnimal bakes the body separately from four articulated legs: each leg retains limbIndex and a hip pivot, with shared material meshes in hip coordinates. Material textures are generated locally. Models use deterministic 128-square clay/wood Canvas textures. The renderer creates deterministic 512-square grass/path color and bump maps; color textures are sRGB and repeat over the ground. Nature uses generated DataTexture alpha maps for cloud shadows and precipitation. Grass/path material roughness, water material properties and static lighting do not currently accumulate rain or change with weather severity. Batching, poses and visibility Static scenery and flock animals have separate submission paths: Path Construction Per-frame submission --- --- --- Static scenery batchScenery() groups eligible terrain pieces by geometry identity + material identity. It skips world-owned geometry and transparent materials; groups smaller than two remain meshes. Tufts/reeds are explicitly instanced. trackScenery() retains immutable source matrices and transformed bounding spheres. cullScenery() compacts visible matrices into the front of each instance buffer and updates count and upload range. Sheep/cows One logical Object3D pose per animal; separate InstancedMesh for every baked body/limb material piece of each species. Original animal indices are stored in the species batch. A conservative species radius includes limb pivot envelopes. The same compacted visibleIndices order feeds every body/leg mesh; gait uses original indices, so disappearing/reappearing animals retain their phase and pose. Pip/helpers Normal articulated scene groups; helper meshes are keyed by helper type. Pip and ground helper legs/tails animate; drone propellers rotate and it bobs above ground. The map is designed for one helper per type. Nature Cloud/body/wing/shadow InstancedMesh objects plus optional precipitation Points. Rewrites instance transforms; disables automatic frustum culling. Nature does not use the renderer's custom instance compaction. The renderer creates its own camera frustum before both scenery and animal culling. Automatic aggregate InstancedMesh frustum culling is disabled for these batches. Scenery spheres receive two extra world units; flock bounds receive three, retaining edge silhouettes and nearby sun shadows. Only the compacted prefix is uploaded. This reduces submitted instances, while CPU work still scans the stored instances/animals each frame. Only batched fence parts cast shadows in the static batching path; other eligible batched scenery does not inherit arbitrary original shadow flags. Flock locomotion uses a stop-motion pose clock, Math.floor(time 12) / 12. Root bob/roll and leg swings depend on speed. The limb matrix order is important: js // Same transform contract used by createAnimal and updateAnimalBatches. const limbWorld = animalWorld.clone() .multiply(new THREE.Matrix4().makeTranslation(...pivot)) .multiply(new THREE.Matrix4().makeRotationX(gait)); World-height samples place Pip, flock and ground helpers on bridges/terrain. Saved animals remain rendered; their heading uses a fixed index-based pose. The rectangular goal marker pulses; goal-view.js derives its surface/outline and barn floor from targetFootprint world vertices. The map projects those same corners rather than drawing a circular goal dot. Celebration allocates 36 short-lived confetti meshes, updated and disposed in the main renderer update. Bark uses barkSurface(level,origin,scale,geometry): it splits expanding ring pieces at deck and individual plank boundaries before assigning surface heights, keeping triangles above land and raised planks even across deck edges. Depth testing remains enabled and depth writes disabled so fences/rails retain normal occlusion. updateBarks(dt) freezes at zero dt, finishes after .6 seconds, reuses dynamic geometry buffers, and disposes expired effects; a hard cap of four and level replacement bound resources. Covered structure visibility StructureCutaway in cutaway.js clones each section material so fades cannot affect shared scenery. It tests camera-facing rays from center/body-width samples of nearby Pip and all flock animals against each section's local bounding box. Body-radius coverage and .18-unit membership hysteresis preserve visibility while partly entering/exiting; exponential blending approaches .14 opacity for obstructing sections and restores other parts. It uses current camera/world matrices, including rotated structures and split groups. Faded panels disable depth writes and shadow casting; opacity/transparency/shadow settings restore when clear. A zero-time update freezes membership/fades. Collision and admission never read this visual state. Tests inspect rays/materials and disposal; they do not prove real transparent draw ordering. Camera and map The camera is orthographic and tilted from (center.x,36,center.z+29) toward (center.x,0,center.z-2). Normal view height is 39 in portrait aspect below .8, otherwise 36; preview height is 50 or 44. Preview follows the spawn. Gameplay follows a weighted dog/unsaved-flock center (or goal when all are saved), with leading space, smoothing, and clamps that retain Pip when a helper moves the flock far away. The sun follows the same center. These clamps are covered by projected-position tests, not physical-device readability checks. The map draws roads, continuous rivers/bridges, world fence segments, goal, every flock animal, Pip and helpers. It scales x and z independently into the map rectangle, so it is a navigation overview rather than a camera projection or a guaranteed equal-scale plan. Resource ownership and level changes Before replacement, setLevel traverses terrain and actors, disposes InstancedMesh instance resources, and disposes geometries/materials explicitly tagged ownedGeometry/ownedMaterial. Label textures are disposed via ownedLabel. It clears groups and batch/helper/particle state, then builds the next world. Cached model geometry/materials and shared fence geometry deliberately survive. Distinct resources are deduplicated with sets before disposal. Nature has an independent resources set. clear() disposes instance resources, removes children, disposes owned geometry/materials/textures, and resets state; setLevel() calls it first. dispose() additionally removes the nature root from the scene. The renderer has no comprehensive public dispose() method for its shared renderer/material/model resources. Bark effects are updated within renderer time and cleared/disposed on level replacement. Structure cutaway restores original materials and shadow flags and disposes its per-panel clones before terrain replacement. Nature: implemented behavior and requested work weatherForLevel(level) accepts a string or {type} override, normalizes unknown types to sun, and disallows snow at indexes 0–7. Default public levels 1–5 are sunny; from level 6 the five-step cycle is sun, wind, rain, sun, snow. Its intensity is fixed at 0 for sun and .3 otherwise. It does not transition weather during a level or consume a requested intensity field. Current implementation Open requested behavior --- --- Five clouds, four sphere blobs each, Lambert material opacity .5; matching soft textured plane shadows drift with wind. F-008 requests soft textured clouds at 10–25% opacity. F-023 requests changing cloud coverage/lighting and bounded lightning/thunder events. Up to eight butterflies (four in rain, zero in snow), five birds and three squirrels. Fixed requested counts, with unsafe spawn slots hidden. F-017 requests recognizable hopping rabbits and random populations scaled to meadow size. Rabbits are absent. Fauna are simple sphere bodies/wings; squirrel tail uses the squirrelWing batch. Idle movement follows a home-centered orbit, with heading from desired movement. F-003 requests detailed squirrels with readable scurry/bound, alert, blocked and turning motion. Current implementation does not establish visual acceptance. Rain has 128 particles; snow has 72. Points follow the dog, fall, and wrap in a bounded local volume. Wind changes drift speed. F-022 requests severity-dependent species retreat/return. Current counts vary by weather type only; there is no shelter/severity state machine. Weather affects decorative motion/particles and audio selection. F-024 requests accumulated wetness, puddles/splashes and rain-responsive rivers. No such surface state exists. Barn has a full segmented pitched roof, short independent wall/trim sections and StructureCutaway. F-016 source is in Review: ray-intersecting sections fade while covering Pip/flock; actual draw-order/readability verification remains. Spawn search is deterministic from the level seed, tries at most 180 candidates per animal, prefers locations beside routes, and requires traversable ground, distance over 5.5 from route centerlines and over 9 from initial threats. Runtime threats include Pip, all flock animals (including saved ones), and helpers. Birds flee within 10 units, other fauna within eight; squirrels use canOccupy and axis fallback, while flying species may cross water/fences. Fauna stay within a two-unit map margin. MeadowNature.update clamps its own interval to .1 seconds; explicit pause returns before any motion or transform updates. The nature layer creates eight instanced batches (cloud, shadow and body/wing for each of three species), plus optional precipitation. Zero-count batches can exist, including butterflies during snow. Tests bound its drawable objects to at most 11; that is a structural limit, not a measured GPU draw/frame-time result. Synthesized audio lifecycle [audio.js](../../src/audio.js) has no fetched samples. start() is intended for a user gesture; it lazily creates one AudioContext and three gain buses (music .42, effects .6, ambience .38) into a master gain (.65, or zero when muted). It awaits context.resume() and returns a success boolean; unsupported/restricted audio fails silently so gameplay can continue. Each activation increments activationGeneration. After asynchronous resume, activation succeeds only if its generation and context remain current and the instance is not disposed. pause(), a later start/resume, or dispose() invalidates pending completions. Main also rechecks mode after awaiting audio start, preserving a modal opened while activation was pending. Mute is independent of this state diagram: it changes master gain, stops ambience immediately, and prevents play()/ambience source creation. It does not stop the music scheduler; music can continue advancing behind zero master gain. Pause stops the scheduler, stops/disconnects ambience, and fades the music bus toward .0001; it leaves the AudioContext running and permits UI effects. Already scheduled music sources are not individually canceled by pause. Resume restarts scheduling at the current sequence step with a fresh initial delay. Music runs at tempo 106 over 128 eighth-note steps: eight bars with C/F/C/G/Am/F/G/C roots and generated bass, pluck and melody voices. _note creates an oscillator/envelope, schedules its stop, and disconnects both on end; marimba adds a quieter upper partial. Effects select short note phrases or the two synthesized bark sweeps. Scheduling is bounded in two ways: there is at most one 100 ms interval, and each callback schedules at most eight steps within a .25 second lookahead. If late, it advances the modulo-128 sequence mathematically past overdue steps instead of creating old oscillators. Invalid tempos return without scheduling. Per-frame ambience creates no interval and never creates an AudioContext. Birds use two or three short sine sweeps with variation; each lasts .17 seconds. Their countdown subtracts at most .25 seconds per ambience update, so a large frame gap cannot replay a backlog. Normal gaps are 4–10 seconds; rain uses 10–20 seconds and suppresses chirps at intensity ≥.7. Bird chirps are independent of visual bird instances/positions. Weather loops one shared two-second generated noise buffer through a low-pass filter; gains scale by clamped intensity (rain .032, snow .012, wind .024 before bus/master gain). Weather changes stop/disconnect the previous chain. Main passes level.weather when present, otherwise the nature weather type, not its complete configuration. Audio defaults missing intensity to .3 and accepts/clamps explicit intensity, whereas nature currently fixes intensity by type. A future shared weather transition contract must resolve this difference rather than assuming both consumers already share severity. dispose() is idempotent and final: it invalidates activation, clears the interval, releases ambience synchronously, stops/disconnects tracked sources, closes the context and drops node/buffer references. Subsequent start() returns false. Verification and known defects These targeted checks run without a browser from the repository root: sh node --test tests/render.test.js tests/nature.test.js tests/audio.test.js [render.test.js](../../tests/render.test.js) constructs actual Three.js geometry/matrices with Canvas and WebGL substitutes. It checks finite poses/cameras, portrait Pip retention, world-resource disposal without disposing shared animal/fence geometry, bounded scenery submissions, conservative instance/shadow retention, bounded route-adjacent detail, animal compaction and articulated pose restoration. Counts are geometry/submission evidence; they do not establish FPS, correct pixels or artifact-free transparency. [nature.test.js](../../tests/nature.test.js) checks deterministic weather bounds/snow gating, safe spawning, fleeing from saved animals/helpers, squirrel occupancy, matching cloud-shadow positions, pause freezing and one-time resource disposal. [audio.test.js](../../tests/audio.test.js) uses fake contexts/parameters/timers to check competing pending activation, pause/dispose races, bounded overdue scheduling, one reusable context/timer/noise buffer, chirp limits, mute/pause effects and cleanup. Those substitutes do not verify browser autoplay behavior, audible timbre or listening balance. F-001/F-002/F-010/F-016 are source-implemented and remain in Review pending independent and applicable pixel/device checks. F-003 squirrel appearance/motion remains Open. Actual rendered motion is still required for levels 4/8 path bends, bridge bark at center/ends/approaches, rotated target corners and covered structures, transparent overlap, pause/restart and phone audio. See [P0/P1 runbook](../validation/P0-P1.md); headless geometry/ray tests do not close temporal flicker or transparency acceptance. Decorative grass and bark composition Decorative instanced grass blades are an opacity-one transparent pass with depth testing enabled and depth writes disabled. They render over the opaque meadow normally. Bark renders afterward (renderOrder=1) while keeping depth testing; opaque fences and bridge rails still supply depth and hide the lower ring. This specifically prevents blade tips from cutting holes in the bark without raising it above rails or disabling scene depth globally. A real Three.js blade/ring/fence ray-and-queue regression supports the policy; actual transparent-pass appearance still requires device rendering."},{"title":"Development & release","url":"development.html","text":"Development, QA and release context [Documentation home](README.md) · [Architecture](ARCHITECTURE.md) · [Issue tracker](ISSUES.md) Local setup Use Node.js 22 or newer and the locked dependencies. Run commands from the repository root. sh npm ci npm run dev -- --host 0.0.0.0 Production checks and static build: npm test npm run build Explicit development distribution with isolated QA controls: npm run build:dev Vite writes dist/; npm run preview -- --host 0.0.0.0 serves that build when a browser-enabled environment is available. Local font assets are included. WebGL 2 is required by the current renderer. Audio requires browser user interaction; initial play or enabling sound initiates activation. Documentation maintenance Markdown in docs/ and the root README is the editable source. Generated HTML is checked in under docs/html/, including local CSS, navigation/search JavaScript and Mermaid. To refresh and validate: sh npm run docs:build npm run docs:check Open docs/html/index.html directly in a browser. The generator resolves links among the documented pages, creates heading anchors and a page table of contents, and emits an offline search index. Documentation checks also parse all Mermaid diagrams and exercise search, focus, copy cleanup and theme rerendering using jsdom; these DOM substitutes do not verify native browser rendering. Edit Markdown, not generated HTML. Regeneration must be deterministic. Keep third-party diagram code and license notices together. Adding a page requires adding it to the generator's explicit document list, then rebuilding and checking links. Test map Tests What they support ------ tests/simulation.test.js Herd delivery, body collision, reward/profile behavior tests/companions.test.js Largest connected herd, holds, attendance, barriers tests/world.test.js Generation, route/footprint validity, clearance and seeded fixtures tests/herding-journey.test.js Entire flocks delivered with ordinary simulated dog inputs in selected levels tests/progression.test.js Tier cost/unlock/activation and legacy data validation tests/ui.test.js Production controller against DOM/audio substitutes; navigation, rewards, restart and input tests/delivery.test.js First-arrival timer, partial score, lifetime earned ledger and legacy migration tests/goal-view.test.js Shared target highlight/border/floor and projected minimap corners tests/world-geometry.test.js Perpendicular crossings, deck/path ratio and shared full-body target footprint tests/surfaces.test.js Ribbon union topology and bark surfaces on land/deck transitions tests/cutaway.test.js Camera-ray section fading, pause, hysteresis and material restoration tests/qa.test.js QA mapping, edit constraints, isolated controller state and build gating tests/deploy-pages.test.js Validated relative assets, canonical staging, redirects and preserved Pages bytes tests/render.test.js Geometry, camera matrices, culling, batches and disposal tests/nature.test.js Wildlife, weather, resource budgets and cleanup tests/audio.test.js Audio activation races, scheduling, ambience and disposal sh Targeted examples; not a substitute for relevant integration checks. node --test tests/ui.test.js tests/audio.test.js node --test tests/world.test.js tests/herding-journey.test.js No screenshot or human listening claim follows from a mocked Canvas/WebGL/WebAudio test. Record real device/browser, revision, scenario and evidence for native touch, scrolling, visibility, audio and GPU performance. Large headless triangle counts are not phone FPS. CI and current publishing behavior The source branch is flock_3D_chatgpt-work. [The workflow](../.github/workflows/deploy-dev2.yml) installs dependencies, checks documentation/tests, builds release and verifies QA exclusion, then builds the development game and verifies QA inclusion before uploading dist/. Its artifact upload does not publish Pages. GitHub Pages uses the separate gh-pages branch and its native deployment workflow. F-025 is complete. The helper stages the game under dev2/ for https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/ and replaces flock/dev2/ with a redirect preserving query/fragment. It validates the emitted relative asset dependency graph before changing a Pages worktree, rejects unsafe/missing assets and build symlinks, and preserves existing root .nojekyll bytes. It uses a locally fetched origin/gh-pages and never fetches, commits or pushes. The verified migration Pages commit is baca95c7ba618d736730194b433254bf5058dd66: all 15 game files and the redirect were hash-verified, and 17 unrelated blobs stayed unchanged. The live migration contains the preceding game revision; do not infer that the new P1 source batch is deployed. See [validation evidence](validation/P0-P1.md). npm run dev and npm run build:dev enable QA; normal npm run build removes the dynamic QA branch/module and its mutation handlers. CI packages the checked development build after checking both modes. node scripts/check-build-mode.mjs release rejects QA chunks/signatures in a release artifact; node scripts/check-build-mode.mjs development requires the QA JavaScript/CSS and expected entry/export signatures. For a development publication, use the checked final build:dev artifact. Release checklist 1. Read the latest issue/requirements decisions and fetch source and gh-pages. Require a clean intended source revision; do not overwrite another publisher's newer Pages head. 2. Pass relevant tests and both build modes when QA is affected. Rebuild the intended final distribution (build:dev for this development game); inspect QA presence/absence in each bundle. Stage only the intended dev2/ output and any explicitly agreed old-URL redirect. Preserve the root, existing flock/dev/ and unrelated content. Do not alter saved profile keys. 3. Review the Pages-tree diff against the latest parent. Verify relative asset paths and the /Flock/ repository URL prefix. Record the source SHA associated with the build. 4. Commit/push Pages with a checked expected parent/ref. Wait for successful native Pages deployment, not just successful source CI. 5. Fetch the new live HTML and every generated asset; compare bytes/hashes with the build. Run real-browser smoke checks when available. Record Pages SHA, workflow result, canonical URL and any validation limits. Documentation HTML is a repository deliverable in docs/html/; it is not automatically copied into the game build or published as another Pages site. A future hosted docs URL needs an explicit publishing location and preserved-content review. Agent handoff Use isolated worktrees for parallel owned files; integrate commits only after inspection. Blind reviewers should receive the candidate, task/requirements and scope, not historical scores or desired wording. Record confirmed problems, concrete evidence and checks. A threshold score is not a reason to relabel unverified browser behavior as tested. See [P0/P1 runbook](validation/P0-P1.md) for concrete manual scenarios. F-014 remains Blocked without a physical phone. The installed Sites instruction says “If $control-browser is unavailable, skip browser QA” (/root/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sites/1.0.0/skills/sites/SKILL.md); capability discovery found no browser tools, so this session supplies no browser screenshots or native-device claim. Before ending a session, update the issue execution record, regenerate HTML, run documentation checks, commit/push the intended branch and state exactly what was deployed. Do not close open features just because their requirements were documented."},{"title":"P0 / P1 validation","url":"p0-p1-validation.html","text":"P0/P1 validation and release runbook Recorded 14 October 2026. [Documentation home](../README.md) · [Tracker](../ISSUES.md) · [Quality history](../QUALITY.md). Public levels are 1–100; source indexes are 0–99. This record separates implemented source, publication and physical-device evidence. Current evidence and status Scope Evidence Boundary --------- P0 / F-025 Helper 6616c41; canonical Pages migration baca95c7ba618d736730194b433254bf5058dd66; all 15 game files and legacy redirect hash-verified, 17 unrelated blobs unchanged; independent blind medium review 9/10. Done for bounded migration. Published game is the preceding revision, not this P1 batch. Source/HTTP checks do not establish phone play or storage continuity. F-001/F-002 Surface source eed14e5: ribbon union and bark deck/plank splitting, normal depth testing, pause-aware bounded effects. Review: geometry tests do not establish pixels, temporal flicker or visible rail occlusion. Record final integrated regression results after all source fixes. F-005 World source 4887ea7: perpendicular sampled-river crossings, straight approaches, 1.25 × usable-road deck width and shared collision/render descriptors. Review: cow clearance and simulated journeys are bounded source evidence, not human/device acceptance. F-009 Delivery source 1fee206: first-arrival recovery timer, proportional partial score, positive unlock, v2 lifetime earned ledger and legacy migration. Review: formula/pacing need independent and human assessment. F-010 World 4887ea7, goal view 984fc64: shared rotated rectangle for walls, full-body admission, highlight/border, barn floor and minimap. Review: geometry agreement does not prove rendered appearance. F-016 Cutaway 4cf9719: body-sample camera rays, segmented roof/walls, fade hysteresis, material/shadow restoration. Review: real transparent draw ordering and readability remain unverified. F-018 QA df1d193, completed-resume fix 7acc632: development gating, isolated session, paused animal edits, safe bolt edits, level jump, reset/export. Review: QA-enabled publication and physical touch checks remain pending. F-014 No physical phone available. Browser capability discovery exposed no usable browser tools. Blocked. No browser/device screenshot, Safari gesture, listening or GPU claim is made. Historical 9.1 technical / 8.6 gameplay scores in QUALITY.md belong to the earlier bounded game review. P0's 9/10 belongs only to the migration. No new overall P1/game score is recorded here. All P2 items remain Open. Installed Sites guidance is: “If $control-browser is unavailable, skip browser QA” (source: /root/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sites/1.0.0/skills/sites/SKILL.md). Browser QA was skipped under that instruction; physical-phone F-014 remains blocked independently of headless source checks. Repeatable source and bundle checks Run from the intended integrated revision with Node 22+ and locked dependencies: sh npm test npm run build node scripts/check-build-mode.mjs release npm run build:dev node scripts/check-build-mode.mjs development After Markdown integration and generator page registration: npm run docs:build npm run docs:check Record command exit status, source SHA and actual test totals in the final release record. Do not copy intermediate totals into a final claim. build and build:dev both replace dist/; rebuild the intended final artifact before preparing Pages. CI checks release exclusion first, then checks/packages the explicit development build with QA. The artifact inspector rejects QA chunk/signature presence in release, and requires QA JavaScript/CSS plus expected signatures in development. Targeted checks identify useful ownership boundaries: sh node --test tests/surfaces.test.js tests/world-geometry.test.js tests/goal-view.test.js node --test tests/delivery.test.js tests/ui.test.js tests/progression.test.js node --test tests/cutaway.test.js tests/render.test.js tests/qa.test.js node --test tests/deploy-pages.test.js tests/herding-journey.test.js These tests use actual production modules and real Three.js geometry/matrices behind substituted browser/graphics/audio surfaces. Synthetic position fixtures verify state/geometry and are distinct from ordinary-input herding journeys. None measures actual rendered pixels, phone FPS, human timing or sound quality. Device and browser scenarios to run For each case record device, OS/browser/version, portrait dimensions, source SHA, build mode, Pages SHA if live, public level/seed, expected and observed outcome, and screenshot/video where relevant. Mark unavailable evidence as pending. Capture motion for flicker, transitions and occlusion; a still image cannot establish temporal correctness. Scenario Reproduction and expected behavior ------ Canonical entry/migration Fresh-load/reload https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/; visit legacy /Flock/flock/dev2/?qa=checkmeadow. The legacy page must replace itself with canonical URL retaining query/fragment; assets load locally and there is one maintained game copy. Verify normal saved progress on the same origin. F-001 paths Public levels 4 and 8, default and alternate seeds/directions. Follow gentle/sharp corners, reversals and branch joins while moving camera. Textured road/shoulder remains continuous without dark triangles, spikes, holes or flicker. F-002 bark Land, each deck center, both deck ends and dry approaches; bark rings straddling deck/land at small/large radii. Ring stays above grass/path/planks; fences and rails still occlude it. Pause freezes effect age; resume finishes; repeated calls and meadow replacement leave no lingering rings. F-005 crossings Default and four seeded directions, early/late levels and alternate routes. Check deck axis against local stream tangent, aligned straight approach/departure, width 1.25 × usable road (shoulder excluded), cow/Buddy traversal and dry turning room. Re-run level-2 full-flock retrieval after changes. F-010 targets Pen/field/barn, each compass rotation and a non-cardinal synthetic fixture. Compare highlight/border, enclosure corners, barn floor and map polygon. Bring full bodies into safe corners beyond former circular goal; edge-straddling bodies must not count. Approach through the 7-unit opening; walls still block bodies. F-009 travel/first arrival Wait before delivery: no deadline starts. A parked animal without Pip must not count. First attended valid arrival starts exactly one 30–60 second timer with full initial allowance. Later arrivals do not reset it. HUD count and score/possible score remain consistent. F-009 finalization Deliver all early, then separately time out with a partial flock. Full delivery finishes immediately; timeout finalizes before late movement/admission. Results freeze delivered count and score. Pause/menu/workshop/background/QA do not consume countdown. Restart/replay clears transient timer. F-009 reward ledger Tutorial two of five: score 32/80, next meadow open, +32 bolts. Spend/reload; equal/worse replay pays zero; improve to four of five: score 64/80, +32; improve to five: +16. Reapply result: zero. Legacy full completion loads fully earned without another payout. Zero deliveries must not pay/unlock (synthetic state fixture; ordinary timeout requires first arrival). F-016 under cover Barn, rotated views, Pip alone, near-camera animal, partial doorway entry/exit and split flock. Only obstructing camera-facing roof/wall sections fade smoothly and restore after leaving. Pause freezes fades. Check bark/weather/cloud overlap, shadows and readable distant walls; collision/admission remain intact. F-018 level/currency Confirm local/dev distribution shows QA and production does not. QA 0 and 1 both reach tutorial; 100 reaches final campaign level. No unlock restriction. Add/remove safe whole bolts; zero floor, invalid/fractional/overflow inputs rejected. Reload restores normal save rather than test progress. F-018 animal editing Open QA while countdown runs: simulation/input/audio/countdown freeze. Select animal by species/ID, edit X/Z with mouse and touch, verify finite coordinates/bounds and body occupancy rejection. Explicit override permits collision-invalid in-bounds repros; out-of-bounds remains rejected. Saved state stays saved and velocity resets. Resume uses active-world edits; a completed meadow returns to results without another award. Reset starts fresh tutorial/profile; export JSON captures seed/world/dog/profile/animals. No normal-save writes. F-014 controls/audio Portrait thumb steering, double-tap bark without zoom, modal focus and workshop scrolling; pause/background/resume audio, sound toggle, real music/bird/weather balance; restart cancel/confirm and persistence. Record actual phone evidence before changing Blocked status. QA is a coordinate-form editor, not a drag-to-place map tool. Export is a downloadable reproduction record; there is no import control. Editing delivered animals preserves their delivered state. These limits should be stated when sharing a scenario. Publish this source batch separately 1. Fetch latest source/Pages references, require a clean intended source revision and record its SHA. Pass relevant checks; rebuild build:dev for the requested QA-enabled development distribution. 2. Prepare a fresh absolute worktree path with node scripts/deploy-pages.mjs /tmp/flock-pages-p1-review. Review full staged diff against its Pages parent, including canonical/legacy scope, unchanged unrelated blobs and existing root marker bytes. 3. Publish using a checked expected Pages parent; record the Pages SHA and successful native Pages workflow. A source CI artifact upload is not publication. 4. Fetch live entry and every generated file, compare hashes with the intended final dist/, verify legacy redirect and preserve unrelated Pages blobs. Record file totals for this particular build; the earlier migration's 15-file total is not the expected new build count. 5. Append actual independent review findings, test/build/doc outcomes and live verification evidence. Keep Review/Blocked items where pixel/device acceptance remains unavailable. Do not re-label old scores as new P1 scores. At this snapshot the source batch is reviewable but its final integrated checks and separate publication are not recorded as complete. This page is a runbook, not evidence that pending scenarios ran. Integration checks before final review The first integrated run found Float32-collapsed path slivers at level 4 seed 9182 and alternate level-8 routes. The fix tests area and winding after GPU-coordinate quantization, retaining neighboring coverage. The complete integrated source suite then passed 156/156 before the final grass-depth regression was added. QA/main integration also verifies timer freezing, no normal-save writes, and restoration of completed results without a second reward. Final totals, blind follow-up scores and P1 deployment verification are recorded below when available. Completed blind review loop and final local checks The first P1 gameplay and technical reviewers were fresh agents with separate detached worktrees and medium effort. They were instructed not to inspect Git history, writer rationale, sibling findings, prior review documents or score targets. The technical critic accidentally encountered an unrelated historical game score in a broad requirements search and reported disregarding it; no prior P1 score was supplied. Follow-ups by the same critics are rechecks, not new blind first passes. Review First pass at a6adfc6 Follow-up at f0a87a1 Confirmed issues resolved ------:---:--- Gameplay 9.0/10 9.4/10 QA resume of a completed scenario now restores results without a second award Technical 8.5/10 9.0/10 Decorative grass no longer writes depth over bark; fences/rails retain occlusion. CI now packages the QA-enabled development build Both follow-ups found no new confirmed defect. The gameplay critic independently exercised full and partial completion, three QA reopen/resume cycles, unchanged rewards/elapsed time, no normal-save writes and Next to level 2 (29 focused tests). The technical critic executed Three’s render-list queue and actual geometry/depth checks, verified release/dev mode checks (including expected failures for the wrong mode), and passed 19 focused scene/surface/controller tests. The infinite cutaway ray beyond a synthetic camera near plane was identified as an unreachable robustness limitation under the fixed gameplay camera, not a present defect. Root final checks passed 157/157 source tests. Both compiled distributions pass scripts/check-build-mode.mjs: release 15 files/0 QA assets, development 17 files/2 QA assets. Documentation is regenerated with the validation page and local links/anchors checked, plus nine real Mermaid sources and four DOM behavior tests. No GPU screenshots, phone frame rate, native gestures or actual phone listening were verified. Development agents used medium effort for deployment, surfaces, cutaway and QA; high effort for shared world geometry and delivery/save migration. The integrated Float32 geometry defect was escalated to a separate high-effort fix. Documentation was updated after integrated code was ready. New P2/P3 requests from remote commit 6ac8eb7 are preserved as F-027 wildlife fence/edge traversal and F-026 Quit; they were not implemented in this batch."},{"title":"Issue tracker","url":"issues.html","text":"FLOCK — Remaining issues and change tracker Updated: 14 October 2026 (America/Vancouver). Repository: novydhillon/Flock. Working branch: flock_3D_chatgpt-work. Source checkpoint: integrated P0/P1 batch (delivery 1fee206, goal view 984fc64; see execution record). Canonical live game: https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/. F-025 migration is published and verified at Pages baca95c7ba618d736730194b433254bf5058dd66; it contains the preceding game revision. The new P1 source batch has passed blind source review and awaits its own publication/verification. This tracker distinguishes source implementation from visual/device acceptance and live release. How agents should use this document 1. Read this tracker, README and docs/QUALITY.md; inspect current code and branch state before starting. Public level numbers here are one-based; code indexes are zero-based. 2. Claim an issue by recording owner, branch/worktree, status and date. Use isolated worktrees for parallel implementation; coordinate ownership of shared files. Do not mark screenshot reports reproduced from a still image alone. 3. Confirm design decisions listed under each issue before coding dependent behavior. Explicit user requirements take precedence over proposed defaults. Preserve portrait, single-thumb play and the cheerful art direction. 4. Record reproduction level/seed, expected versus observed behavior, implementation commit, meaningful checks, reviewer findings and remaining limits. Use statuses: Open → In progress → Review → Done, or Blocked with the reason. 5. Run relevant regression checks and the production build. Use fresh blind reviewers for a substantial gameplay/rendering batch; give them requirements and the candidate, without earlier scores or implementation rationale. Fix confirmed defects and recheck. Do not claim visual or phone validation from headless tests. 6. A completed source change and a live release are separate milestones. Record deployment commit and verification separately. Resolve F-025 before the next game release: publish to dev2/ on the latest gh-pages tree, giving the public URL /Flock/dev2/. The old flock/dev2/ destination is superseded. Preserve unrelated content and handle the old URL explicitly as part of migration. Changes to this tracker alone do not require a game deployment. Priority: P0 = highest priority; complete before other queued work. P1 = correctness/gameplay foundation; P2 = planned feature/polish; P3 = broader validation/optimization. All open items are unassigned until explicitly claimed. Work overview ID Priority Type Item Status Depends on ------------------ F-025 P0 Deployment Correct public deployment URL to /Flock/dev2/ Done Verified migration F-001 P1 Reported defect Path-corner polygon flicker Review — F-002 P1 Reported defect Bark effect above ground and bridge deck Review — F-003 P2 Art/behavior Detailed squirrel; species-appropriate motion; no spinning Open — F-004 P2 Art Flowing-water texture Open — F-005 P1 Geometry/gameplay Perpendicular crossings; bridges 25% wider than paths Review — F-006 P2 Gameplay Gates requiring player opening from level 20 Open F-005 F-007 P2 Environment Stone walls on selected levels Open F-006 interaction contract F-008 P2 Art Soft textured clouds at 10–25% opacity Open — F-009 P1 Gameplay/economy Arrival countdown; partial-delivery scoring Review F-010 F-010 P1 Correctness Goal shape matches enclosure Review — F-011 P2 Feedback Blue dog-tail light; red bark flash Open F-002 shared bark event F-012 P2 Gameplay Level-gated wanderers and short bolting Open F-005, F-009 balance F-013 P2 Known review concern Level-5 pacing spike Open F-005; retest after F-009/F-012 F-014 P1 Validation gap Real-phone controls, appearance and sound Blocked Physical phone unavailable F-015 P3 Validation gap Mobile GPU performance and transition stability Open Rendering batches F-016 P1 Visibility Camera-facing barn/covered-structure cutaway Review F-010 shared enclosure geometry F-017 P2 Wildlife Detailed hopping rabbits; random size-scaled wildlife counts Open F-003, F-022 F-018 P1 QA tooling Dev-only level, currency and animal-placement menu Review — F-019 P2 Environment Add bushes Open — F-020 P2 Environment Increase tree size Open — F-021 P2 Art Richer bush/tree detail and textures Open F-019, F-020 F-022 P2 Wildlife/weather Species retreat as weather worsens Open F-017; shared weather state with F-023/F-024 F-023 P2 Weather/feedback Weather transitions, clouds, lighting, lightning and thunder Open F-008 F-024 P2 Weather/surfaces Accumulating wetness, puddles, splashes and river response Open F-004; shared weather state with F-023 F-026 P3 UI Pause-menu Quit returns to landing page Open — F-027 P2 Wildlife Wildlife can pass through fences and leave/enter the game area Open F-017 Screenshot evidence Picture numbers refer to the user's attachment order, including the duplicate uploads. Picture Filename Evidence visible / reported --------- 1 IMG_8372.jpeg / IMG_8372(1).jpeg Level 4, 10/14 delivered; black triangular/striped artifacts at path corners. User reports flickering. 2 IMG_8367.jpeg / IMG_8367(1).jpeg Level 8; bark ring is visible around but hidden beneath bridge planks. 3 IMG_8368.jpeg / IMG_8368(1).jpeg Level 9; squirrel appears as a simple body and tail. User reports spinning in place. 4 IMG_8364.jpeg / IMG_8364(1).jpeg Level 8; black triangular artifacts at winding path bends near crossing. The images are visible in the conversation. Their advertised local attachment paths were unavailable during earlier reads; binaries are not embedded in this repository. Preserve these filenames and descriptions when handing off. Static images establish visible artifacts, not their temporal behavior or root cause. Acceptance criteria and implementation notes F-025 — Correct the deployment URL (P0 — highest priority) Requirement: deploy the standalone game at https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/ instead of https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/flock/dev2/. This issue takes precedence over all other open work. Accept when: the current standalone build loads directly at the required URL, including on a fresh visit/reload, with all JavaScript, styles, fonts and other assets resolving successfully beneath /Flock/dev2/. The gh-pages output directory is dev2/, not flock/dev2/; /Flock/ is GitHub Pages' repository URL prefix, not an extra output directory. Verify deployed file hashes against the intended production build and record the Pages commit/workflow result and final URL. Implementation scope: inspect and update scripts/deploy-pages.mjs, deployment/build configuration, README, tracker instructions and any other hard-coded publish destinations or game links. Keep relative asset support. Preserve the Pages root, existing flock/dev/ and all unrelated files. Do not clear or rename saved-profile keys as part of this URL correction; verify existing progress remains available at the new path on the same origin. Migration decision: explicitly record what happens to the previous /Flock/flock/dev2/ URL (for example, a redirect to the canonical URL). Do not leave two independently maintained game copies or delete unrelated legacy content. Any redirect must retain working navigation and use the correct repository prefix. Done evidence: helper source 6616c41; published Pages commit baca95c7ba618d736730194b433254bf5058dd66; canonical entry and all 15 game files plus redirect hash-verified; 17 unrelated blobs unchanged. Legacy address redirects with query/fragment retained. Independent blind medium review: 9/10 for the bounded P0 migration. This does not score or deploy the P1 batch, verify real pixels or establish phone storage continuity. See [validation](validation/P0-P1.md). F-001 — Path-corner polygon flicker Requirement: eliminate polygon flickering at path corners (pictures 1 and 4). Accept when: sharp and gentle turns have continuous textured coverage without black triangles, overlapping flicker, holes or abrupt spikes during camera movement. Check default levels 4 and 8 and multiple seeds/directions on a real renderer. Review / source: eed14e5 replaces stacked ribbon pieces with convex-stroke union, disjoint upward triangles and world UVs in surfaces.js; topology/coverage tests include turns, reversals, crossings and reported levels 4/8. Real-renderer flicker/pixel checks remain required. Do not treat topology evidence alone as visual closure. F-002 — Bark effect above ground and bridge deck Requirement: bark remains visible above path, grass, ground and bridge planks; fences and railings still occlude it (picture 2). Accept when: bark works on land, center/end of each bridge and both approaches, with no deck clipping and normal occlusion by raised obstacles. Do not force the ring over every object by disabling depth testing globally. Review / source: eed14e5 splits bark geometry at deck and plank boundaries and lifts each piece above its local surface, with depth testing retained. Renderer-owned .6-second lifetime freezes at zero dt, reuses buffers and caps effects at four; replacement clears owned resources. Tests compare against real bridge geometry behind a substituted renderer. Actual fence/rail occlusion and clipping views remain pending. F-003 — Squirrel detail and movement Requirement: recognizable, more detailed squirrel; it must not spin in place, and must move like a squirrel (expanded user report, 14 October 2026). Accept when: distinct head/ears, face, legs/paws and bushy tail remain readable at game scale; heading follows actual movement, with stable idle/blocked poses and believable short turns. Wildlife still avoids Pip, the robotic flock and helpers and obeys ground collision. Use recognizable squirrel scurrying/bounding, brief alert pauses and coordinated legs/body/tail motion, rather than a sliding body or continuous circular orbit. Observe idle, blocked, turning and fleeing squirrels, not only open-field movement. Real-renderer observation is needed to judge motion quality. Files: src/nature.js; reusable art may belong in src/models.js. Current heading follows desired direction even when occupancy blocks movement; investigate that distinction without treating it as a proved sole cause. F-004 — Flowing water Requirement: add water texture conveying running water. Accept when: subtle moving ripples/flow follow each river's local direction across bends; bridges remain clear, banks have no seams, and water continues beyond map bounds. Pause freezes animation. Reuse/dispose resources and assess mobile cost. Collision stays tied to the river geometry. Files: src/render.js, src/world.js, renderer tests. F-005 — Bridge and crossing alignment Requirement: bridges and path segments crossing water are perpendicular to the river at the crossing. Bridge width is 1.25 × crossing path width. Minimize unnecessary bridge extension into dry ground so animals do not get stuck. Accept when: orientation uses the local river tangent, not a global axis; approaching/exiting paths align with the deck; mesh, terrain height, deck occupancy and rails share the same footprint. Retain enough landing overlap to cross both banks safely. Validate cow-sized bodies, helpers, the full initial flock, alternative routes, all campaign levels and representative seeds. Files: src/world.js, src/render.js, src/models.js, world/journey tests. Preserve the solved level-2 retrieval case; narrower replacement geometry must not reintroduce its stall. The implementation compares usable road width, excluding decorative shoulders. 4887ea7 aligns decks and straight approaches perpendicular to sampled river segments; deck width is 1.25 × road width, including the widened level-2 road/deck. Geometry and journey regression checks support Review; rendered landings remain pending. F-006 — Operable gates from level 20 Requirement: introduce gates that need to be opened on higher levels (20+). Accept when: closed gates visibly and physically block dog/flock/helpers; an accessible single-thumb action opens them; geometry, collision, navigation and minimap agree on state. Levels 1–19 retain their existing accessible openings. Generated routes remain solvable and restart resets gate state. Decision to resolve: proximity action, auto-open interaction or dedicated contextual button; whether gates close again. Do not add a second-thumb requirement. Current gate openings do not implement this requested closed/open state. F-007 — Stone wall variants Requirement: use stone walls instead of fences on some levels. Accept when: selected deterministic levels have readable stone boundaries, consistent visible/physical footprints and usable entrances. Define whether walls block herding signals and how gates join them; raised walls should occlude effects correctly. Keep goals and route clearance valid. Decision to resolve: level/theme distribution and wall height. Candidate files: src/world.js, src/models.js, src/render.js, collision tests. F-008 — Soft textured clouds Requirement: clouds may be overlapping blurred soft-edge blob textures instead of models, remaining semi-transparent at 10–25%. Accept when: soft blobs form moving puffy clouds without hard edges or obvious planes; they do not obscure play. Preserve drifting movement and soft ground shadows, pause behavior and resource cleanup. Validate overlapping transparency and mobile rendering. Working interpretation to confirm: material/composited opacity 0.10–0.25 (not 75–90% opacity). Avoid stacking blobs into a much darker or opaque cloud. Current cloud material opacity is .5; see src/nature.js. F-009 — Delivery countdown and score Requirement: the first flock animal entering the goal starts a 30–60 second countdown, chosen by level complexity. End the level when time reaches zero or the last animal arrives. Score reflects animals delivered and displays score / possible score. Accept when: a single first valid delivery starts one timer; no pre-arrival timer; duration stays within 30–60 seconds; all-delivered ends early; timeout produces a partial result. Final counts and score settle once, late arrivals cannot change the finalized result, pause freezes gameplay time, and replay/restart clears transient state. Display delivered count and earned/possible score consistently in play and results. Preserve player-attended delivery; helpers cannot complete the level alone. Review / decisions recorded 14 October 2026: user approved positive partial unlock, floor(reward saved / total) score/earned entitlement and lifetime best improvement paying only its delta. 1fee206 implements a 30–60 second complexity formula, first-arrival start, timeout-before-admission, early full completion, consistent HUD/results and v2 ledger migration. Legacy completions become fully earned without crediting points again. Replay spending/reload cannot farm duplicate rewards. See [Gameplay](systems/GAMEPLAY.md) for exact formula. Formula balance and human pacing remain review tasks. Files: src/simulation.js, src/main.js, src/progression.js, UI/style and persistence tests. Depends on F-010 so timer starts only inside the correct goal. F-010 — Goal shape matches enclosure Requirement: goal areas follow their pen, barn or fenced-field enclosure shape. Accept when: highlight, valid delivery region, minimap and enclosure footprint agree for rotated destinations; animals outside the wall or near a circular corner cannot score. Entrance remains clear and animal body radius is handled consistently. Check all three destination types across seeds. Review / source: world 4887ea7 exports rotated target local/world transforms, footprint and full-body containment. Goal view 984fc64 uses footprint corners for highlight, border, barn floor and minimap. Wall endpoints derive from the same shape. Body-contracted admission includes safe rectangular corners and rejects edge-straddling bodies. Rotated pen/field/barn geometry tests support the contract; actual rendered comparison remains pending. F-011 — Dog-tail light feedback Requirement: dog's tail light is blue normally and flashes red on bark. Accept when: Pip's blue light visibly flashes red for the bark feedback interval and returns blue reliably. Repeated bark, cooldown, pause and level restart leave no stuck-red state. Decide whether Buddy dog uses the same convention when it has an actual bark event. Do not recolor every shared cyan robotic material. Files: src/models.js, src/render.js, src/main.js; use the same bark event as F-002. F-012 — Wandering and bolting animals Requirement: up to 15% of animals may have a 33% chance to move randomly away from the flock when not actively herded. This begins at level 8. Such an animal has up to 10% chance to bolt away for 1–3 seconds; bolting begins after level 16 (level 17 onward). Accept when: levels 1–7 have no new wandering; levels 1–16 have no bolting. Eligible cohort never exceeds 15% of the flock; document integer rounding. Probabilities are tied to defined behavior opportunities/time, never rolled every frame. No fleeing through rails, walls, river or map fences; saved animals never wander. Active herding suppresses this behavior, and brief bolts end within the specified range. Pause/restart reset behavior safely and seeded tests exercise gates, duration, collision and statistical bounds without flaky expectations. Decisions required: selection/rounding, probability evaluation interval, wander distance/speed/cooldown, what counts as active herding (Pip versus companions), and whether the 10% is conditional on a wander event. Do not silently interpret a chance as a per-second or per-frame rate. Balance together with F-009 to avoid unfair late losses. Files: src/simulation.js, src/world.js, companion/journey/simulation tests. F-013 — Known level-5 pacing concern The fresh gameplay critic's unchanged input controller completed default levels 1–5 in 18.2, 17.2, 28.7, 38.7 and 208.0 simulated seconds. Level 5's jump warrants human playtesting; it is not proof of an impossible level or a measured human time. Accept when: document human attempts, retrieval/chokepoint behavior and difficulty progression; adjust only where evidence supports it. Re-run the existing ordinary-input journey policies after crossing/countdown/wandering changes. Avoid solving pacing by weakening collision or allowing autonomous completion. F-014 — Real-phone verification gap Blocked: no actual physical phone is available for portrait readability, thumb control feel, native Safari double-tap zoom prevention, modal focus/scrolling, actual music/bird/weather balance, or rendered visual defects. Capability discovery found no browser tools. Installed Sites guidance is “If $control-browser is unavailable, skip browser QA” (/root/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sites/1.0.0/skills/sites/SKILL.md). Browser QA was skipped; headless checks cannot close this issue. See [manual runbook](validation/P0-P1.md). Accept when: record device/browser, tested source/deployment revision, scenarios, results and screenshot/video evidence. Include restart confirmation/cancellation/persistence. Existing user screenshots provide evidence of specific visual issues; they do not validate every input/audio scenario. F-015 — Performance verification gap Real phone GPU frame rate and level-transition memory stability remain unmeasured. The existing bounded submission/resource tests are useful but do not establish FPS. Historical production JavaScript was roughly 576 kB / 155 kB gzip, with an advisory Vite chunk-size warning; this is an optimization consideration, not a confirmed gameplay bug. Accept when: measure normal/large/split herds, later expansive worlds, weather/cloud effects and repeated transitions on representative phones. Record frame-time distribution, load behavior and resource growth. Set a device-specific performance target before judging success. F-016 — See Pip and the flock under cover Requirement: barns and other covered structures must not obscure nearby Pip or flock animals. When they walk under cover, the roof and wall nearest the camera become transparent so the player can see them. Accept when: camera-facing roof/wall sections fade while occluding Pip or any flock animal beneath the structure, including animals close to the camera, partially entering/exiting and split groups. Select sections from the current camera view, including rotated structures. Restore their normal appearance when no longer obstructing the actors, without flicker or rapid threshold toggles. Keep distant/non-obstructing structure parts readable. Transparency changes visibility only; collision, goal admission and enclosure boundaries remain intact. Review / source: 4cf9719 provides a full segmented barn roof and independent wall/trim panels. StructureCutaway casts body-sample camera rays into panel bounds, uses coverage hysteresis/smoothed fades and restores original materials/shadows on cleanup. Pause freezes fades; collision/admission do not change. Rotated, partial-entry and split actor tests support source behavior; actual transparent draw order against animals, bark/weather/clouds remains unverified. F-017 — Rabbits and variable wildlife populations Requirement: add rabbits alongside squirrels. Rabbits must be recognizably detailed and move like rabbits. Rabbit and squirrel counts are random and depend on level size. Accept when: ears, face, body, hind legs and tail distinguish rabbits at normal portrait zoom. Hopping has coordinated crouch, push-off, airborne and landing poses, with believable idle/turn/flee transitions; avoid sliding or spinning. Both species avoid Pip/flock/helpers and obey ground barriers. Population varies with usable level area and randomness, with bounded counts for performance and repeatable seeded fixtures for testing. Weather visibility is handled by F-022, not by permanently deleting a level's base population. Decisions to resolve: area-to-count ranges/caps, whether either species may be absent, and spawn spacing/habitat. Do not silently replace the requested randomness with a fixed count per level. Files: src/nature.js, src/models.js, wildlife/resource tests. Coordinate squirrel motion work with F-003. F-018 — Dev-build QA menu Requirement: add a QA menu available in development builds. Testers can jump to any level 0–100, add bolts, remove bolts, and pause the simulation while moving individual flock animals to reproduce scenarios such as animals stuck in game-hindering positions. Accept when: the menu is clearly marked as QA and available in the deployed development build as well as the intended local development configuration. Release builds expose no QA entry point or enabled mutation handlers. Testers can choose a level without campaign unlock restrictions, enter a bolt amount to add/remove, pause gameplay and select/reposition a particular animal. Currency remains finite and nonnegative. Selection and placement work with touch and mouse and identify the selected animal clearly. Editing does not accidentally steer Pip, resume the simulation, advance countdowns, award progress or trigger delivery. Resume uses the edited scenario normally. Review / source: df1d193 adds a dynamically imported QA module only in local development/explicit build:dev; normal production excludes the entry and handlers. User approved QA 0 = tutorial shortcut and public 1–100 = campaign indexes 0–99. First entry starts an isolated default test profile; persistence is suppressed until reload. Dialog pauses gameplay/countdowns/audio, makes background controls inert, permits finite safe bolt edits, identifies animals by species/ID, and edits finite coordinates within bounds. Explicit invalid-placement override bypasses occupancy only; velocity resets and saved state is retained. Reset starts fresh; export downloads a scenario JSON. 7acc632 restores results when resuming a completed QA meadow without another award. Mouse/touch forms exist but physical touch validation and QA-enabled live publication remain pending. Files: build configuration, src/main.js, src/style.css, src/simulation.js, controller/profile tests. QA pause must permit manual edits while freezing all normal simulation; gameplay pause alone is insufficient. Exercise development and release builds separately to verify gating. F-019 — Bushes Requirement: add bushes to the environment. Accept when: organic bush clusters vary in shape/placement and fit fields/woodland edges without hiding critical controls, destinations or crossings. Generation preserves safe spawns, flock staging space and route clearance. Explicitly decide whether bushes are decorative or physical; if physical, mesh and collision footprints must agree and all routes remain traversable. Include visibility/culling and resource cleanup checks. Files: src/world.js, src/models.js, src/render.js, scenery/world tests. Coordinate appearance with F-021 rather than duplicating the assets. F-020 — Larger trees Requirement: increase the size of trees. Accept when: trees are visibly larger at the normal camera scale and remain proportionate to Pip, animals and structures. Maintain readable play space and valid routes/entrances. If trunk or canopy dimensions change, update placement clearance, physical footprints where applicable, bounds and shadow/culling data together. Verify that larger near-camera trees do not introduce new actor-visibility problems; F-016 covers structures specifically. Decision to resolve: target scale or size range and variation across levels. src/models.js, src/world.js and src/render.js are likely touch points. F-021 — Bush and tree surface detail Requirement: add more detail and textures to bushes and trees. Accept when: foliage has readable volume, color/leaf variation and softened organic forms; trunks/branches have bark detail. Preserve the established cheerful handmade appearance and consistency between new bushes and larger trees. Avoid flat uniform blobs, repetitive obvious tiling and excessive fine-detail shimmer at portrait scale. Assess shader/texture memory, draw submissions, shadows and mobile performance; shared/cached resources must dispose correctly. Files: src/models.js, src/render.js, scenery/resource tests. Dependencies F-019/F-020 establish the base shapes and size targets. F-022 — Weather-aware wildlife and shelter Requirement: light rain and light wind still allow butterflies, birds, squirrels, rabbits and other wildlife. As weather worsens, smaller animals disappear/hide first; larger animals remain until more extreme conditions, then hide too. Accept when: light conditions do not indiscriminately suppress wildlife. A documented per-species severity threshold/order causes gradual retreat/hiding as weather intensifies, and plausible return after it eases. Avoid abrupt simultaneous despawns, visible animals frozen in place, repeated threshold flicker or frame-rate-dependent population changes. Hiding wildlife still avoids the flock; ground animals never flee through barriers. Define behavior for airborne species and currently supported weather types, including snow. Coordinate shelter/visibility state with population generation in F-017. Decisions required: species size categories, severity thresholds/hysteresis, shelter/exit behavior and return delay. The user supplied the ordering principle, not exact numeric thresholds. Files: src/nature.js, wildlife tests. Consume the same weather severity/transition state as F-023/F-024. F-023 — Worsening-weather atmosphere and storms Requirement: when weather changes toward rain or storms, increase cloud cover and change lighting to signal worsening conditions. If lightning occurs, show flashes and play thunder sounds. Accept when: transitions adjust cloud coverage and ground lighting smoothly, preserving legibility of Pip, flock and goal. Cloud coverage works with F-008's soft semi-transparent cloud requirement; resolve any storm opacity exception explicitly rather than silently overriding it. Lightning flashes and thunder belong to the same bounded event, with sensible audio timing, and do not create uncontrolled flash bursts or stacked audio sources. Mute, pause, backgrounding, replay and level transitions handle storm effects and pending sounds consistently; clean up event resources. Record real-phone visual/listening checks. Decisions required: severity model/transition timing, whether weather changes during a level, lightning frequency/intensity and thunder timing. A new within-level weather system must explicitly define pause/restart/seed behavior; current fixed per-level choices must not be assumed to provide it. Files: src/nature.js, src/render.js, src/audio.js, weather/audio/resource tests. Provide shared rain/wind severity and event state to F-022 and F-024. F-024 — Rain accumulation, puddles and water response Requirement: ground appearance reflects rainfall. Light rain has little effect; sustained heavier rain slowly darkens grass green and dirt tracks brown. Rainstorms produce standing puddles and splashes when animals walk through them. River water also reflects the weather change. Accept when: wetness accumulates with rain amount over simulation time, rather than switching instantly to a fixed dark material. Grass and paths respond distinctly while remaining readable. Storm puddles occupy plausible ground locations without clipping across bridges or raised surfaces, and flock movement through them generates brief localized splashes; stationary animals do not emit continuous splash bursts. River surface appearance responds coherently to rain/storm intensity while retaining directional flow (F-004). These visual effects do not silently alter movement/collision. Pause freezes accumulation and events; restart/level replacement clears transient resources correctly. Preserve mobile performance under large moving flocks. Decisions required: accumulation/saturation and drying rates, puddle formation thresholds/placement, river changes (for example ripples, roughness or color), and whether Pip/helpers/wildlife also splash. Those examples are implementation options, not approved extra mechanics. Files: src/nature.js, src/render.js, src/world.js, weather/renderer/resource tests. Use shared weather state from F-023 and check material sharing does not leak wetness into the next level. F-026 — Quit to landing page Requirement: the pause menu should include a Quit button that returns to the landing page. Added directly on GitHub while this batch was in progress; retained as an Open P3 item. Accept when: Quit returns to the intro/landing screen with movement stopped and transient gameplay/audio handled consistently. Normal saved progress and earned money are preserved. Define whether the current in-progress meadow is discarded or can be resumed; do not treat Quit as Restart Game. F-027 — Wildlife crossing fences and map edges Requirement: wildlife can pass through fences and leave and enter the game area. Added directly on GitHub as a second F-025; recorded here as F-027 to preserve the existing P0 deployment issue ID. Accept when: the wildlife movement/spawn rules explicitly support crossing fences and departing/returning at the playable perimeter without permitting the robotic flock or Pip to cross their physical boundaries. Coordinate population budgets and natural entry/exit behavior with F-017. Decide how ground wildlife handles rivers, other obstacles and weather shelter; do not silently remove all wildlife collision checks. Previously repaired — retain as regressions These are not open defects unless reproduced again: drone selecting the largest herd and holding when Pip leaves; no unattended goal completion; unlocks (drone 10, Buddy 20); path variety/map extent/continuous rivers; scenery hill collision alignment; pending audio activation overriding pause; overdue music scheduling burst; the default level-2 bridge stall; full-game restart persistence and delayed-audio focus. See docs/QUALITY.md and current tests for scope and limitations. The latest Restart Game source passed CI tests/build and deployment byte verification. Historical blind scores 9.1 technical / 8.6 gameplay describe the earlier bounded review, not a review of these newly requested changes. Do not update scores without a new independent assessment. Execution record Copy one row per claimed issue; retain older entries when reopening work. Issue Owner Status Branch/worktree Source commit Checks and review evidence Deployment commit Date / next action ------------------------ F-025 P0 implementation/release Done Integrated source 6616c41 Asset graph/helper tests; all 15 live game files + redirect hashes; 17 unrelated blobs preserved; independent blind medium 9/10 baca95c7ba618d736730194b433254bf5058dd66 14 Oct 2026; P1 release separate F-001 P1 surfaces Review Integrated source eed14e5 Union topology/coverage regressions; final integrated checks tracked in runbook Not yet published Real-renderer flicker check pending F-002 P1 surfaces Review Integrated source eed14e5 Surface/deck geometry, pause and ownership checks Not yet published Rendered clipping/occlusion pending F-005 P1 world Review Integrated source 4887ea7 Perpendicular/ratio/cow-route and ordinary-input journey checks Not yet published Device/render landing checks pending F-009 P1 delivery Review Integrated source 1fee206 Timer, proportional score, canonical ledger/migration and controller checks Not yet published Independent review and human pacing pending F-010 P1 world/presentation Review Integrated source 4887ea7, 984fc64 Rotated corners, full-body containment, goal/map/floor geometry Not yet published Rendered matching pending F-016 P1 cutaway Review Integrated source 4cf9719 Camera rays, rotations, partial coverage, pause and clone cleanup Not yet published Actual transparency/device views pending F-018 P1 QA Review Integrated source df1d193 Isolated session, mapping, pause/edit validation, bundle gating Not yet published Live QA distribution and touch checks pending F-014 Device QA Blocked — — No physical phone or discovered browser capability; QA skipped per installed Sites guidance — Resume with actual device/revision evidence Decisions and session handoff Record approved answers to the decisions above here, with date and source. At session end list changed issue IDs, remaining blockers, commands/results, branch heads and the next reproducible action. A blocked design decision does not prevent independent visual repairs. Current handoff, 14 October 2026: F-025 migration is Done. Review and release the P1 source batch separately, then record the actual Pages revision and fresh live file hashes. F-001/F-002/F-005/F-009/F-010/F-016/F-018 stay Review; F-014 stays Blocked. All P2 requests remain Open, including expanded squirrel motion, rabbits, vegetation and linked weather systems. Historical reviews are retained as historical evidence, not current game scores. Approved user decisions today: any positive partial delivery opens the next level; earned entitlement is proportional floor score; later lifetime-best improvements pay only the delta; QA 0 shortcuts the tutorial while public campaign remains 1–100. Source balance choices (duration formula, usable road-width comparison/landing overlap) are documented in Gameplay/World and remain subject to review. QA isolation, bounds-limited invalid placement, reset/export and production exclusion are implemented and await applicable device/live checks. See [P0/P1 validation](validation/P0-P1.md) for exact scenarios and limitations. Integration note, 14 October 2026: remote commit 6ac8eb7 added Quit (F-026) and wildlife crossing fences/edges under a duplicate F-025. Both requests are preserved; the latter is now F-027. Neither P2/P3 feature is included in this P0/P1 implementation batch. P1 medium-effort blind review: first pass gameplay 9.0/10 and technical 8.5/10; follow-up at f0a87a1 gameplay 9.4/10 and technical 9.0/10. Confirmed QA-results, grass/bark composition and CI-artifact issues were fixed. Final source checks: 157/157 tests, release/dev artifact mode checks and documentation checks passed. Pixel/device acceptance remains open."},{"title":"Quality record","url":"quality.html","text":"Flock dev2 revision quality record P0/P1 implementation batch — 14 October 2026 Medium-effort blind first passes scored gameplay 9.0/10 and technical 8.5/10. Their follow-up checks of f0a87a1 scored 9.4/10 gameplay and 9.0/10 technical, with no new confirmed defects. The P0 migration separately scored 9/10 and was published at canonical /Flock/dev2/. These are bounded source/headless implementation scores, not verified phone visual quality. Final integrated checks pass 157/157 source tests, release without QA (15 files, zero QA assets), development with QA (17 files, two QA assets), and generated documentation checks. The new P1 build still requires separate publication/hash verification, recorded in [the validation runbook](validation/P0-P1.md). F-014 remains Blocked; visual acceptance for paths, bark and cutaways remains in Review. The earlier review results below are retained as historical records. The historical fresh blind-review retry scored 9.1/10 for technical implementation and 8.6/10 for gameplay implementation after fixes. Neither critic found a remaining confirmed blocking defect in the bounded recheck. The technical review reaches 9/10; the overall game experience remains unverified at that score. Actual phone appearance, sound balance, Safari gestures, touch scrolling, human campaign pacing and GPU performance remain open validation tasks. Review packets excluded README, quality records, writer history and requested score thresholds. Fresh critics independently inspected source and exercised simulation, geometry and controller behavior. An earlier diagnostic gameplay review accidentally read historical scores; it is excluded from the blind final assessment. Review Before fixes Final Confirmed issues addressed ------:---:--- Runtime diagnostic 8.0 — Unattended goal admission, distant rendering workload Fresh source/technical critic 8.4 8.9 Cohesion through fences/trees; unnecessary engine exports; singleton companion hold Fresh gameplay critic 8.4 8.7 Drone treating an isolated singleton as a main herd Fresh technical retry 8.8 9.1 Pending audio activation undoing pause; unbounded overdue music scheduling Fresh gameplay retry 8.2 8.6 Default level-two bridge retrieval stall under the test controller The retry used two new critics with no earlier scores or writer context. The default second meadow initially left all eight sheep unsaved after 400 simulated seconds under the existing test controller. Widening only its first bridge from 8.5 m to 11.5 m lets the same unchanged controller deliver all eight in 17.2 seconds. At that historical snapshot, later early crossings remained 8.5 m, and the map, river and solid rails are retained. Audio activation now uses generation checks so a later pause/disposal/request invalidates an older pending browser resume. Controller startup and sound enabling also respect the current game mode. Delayed music callbacks skip missed beats and cap work; the independent 60-second stall probe schedules one future musical step instead of 212 overdue steps. The gameplay critic independently completed default levels 1–5 with the unchanged legitimate-input controller: 18.2, 17.2, 28.7, 38.7 and 208.0 simulated seconds. The larger jump at level five is a pacing concern for human playtesting, not proof of an impossible level or a measured human completion time. Earlier review findings also drove repairs to dry bridge rail collision, rare seeded spawn clearance, companion clear-ground attendance, workshop focus restoration and wildlife avoidance of delivered animals/helpers. Ground buddies now detour around bridge ends. Cohesion and alignment respect physical barriers. Pip can call over low wood fences/bridge rails, while bodies, companion membership and goal admission use strict ground paths; barns, water and trees still shield Pip's call. Verified checks - 93 automated tests passed at the blind-review retry snapshot. They exercise actual simulation/profile logic, production controller code, real Three.js geometry/matrices, resource ownership and synthesized audio lifecycle. - All routes across 100 default levels are physically traversed by cow-sized bodies. Independent sweeps found no blocked routes or unsafe spawns in 500 additional generated worlds. A separate 1,600-fixture spawn sweep verified the rare-seed repair. - Full original flocks in levels 2, 3, 6, 10 and 16 are delivered across four directions by a bounded simulated player using ordinary dog inputs, safe bridge/pen circling and actual collision. No animal positions or saved flags are changed by that policy. This does not establish full-flock completion of every campaign level, route, seed or player strategy. - Retry critics independently checked 800 and 3,000 generated worlds before the first-bridge widening, with no unsafe actual-radius spawns or cow-clearance route failures. The refreshed default campaign and first-bridge geometry regressions also pass after widening. - Independent integration of actual controller and FarmAudio, with browser surfaces substituted, confirms menu/visibility pause during pending activation settles paused, with zero music schedulers and a ducked music gain. Ten audio lifecycle tests cover both completion orders, mute, disposal and delayed scheduling. - Regression checks cover main-herd selection, isolated-straggler retrieval, fifteen-minute stationary holds, no unattended admission, clear attendance through barriers, singleton standby and reactivation, and exact level-10/20 companion gates with preserved legacy ownership. - Renderer checks establish finite camera/pose matrices, conservative culling, visible shadow retention, exact articulated pose restoration, bounded scenery and deterministic disposal. Level-100 actor submissions are 238,604 triangles with the full flock visible, 80,712 in the tested split view and zero at a distant view. These are geometry/submission counts, not measured FPS or rendered pixels. - Explicit geometry constructor references reduce production JavaScript from about 786 kB/212 kB gzip to 575 kB/154 kB gzip. The remaining Vite chunk-size warning is advisory; no runtime/font asset CDN is required. Verification boundary DOM, Canvas, WebGL and WebAudio substitutes support headless checks. No approved browser/phone environment was available for rendered screenshots, real touchscreen interactions, listening or GPU profiling. The tests therefore do not prove visual polish, native Safari zoom/scroll behavior, perceived audio quality or mobile frame rate. Device priorities are portrait readability and control feel, double-tap bark without page zoom, workshop scrolling, large-flock rendering/level-transition performance, and bird/weather balance through phone speakers. These checks remain necessary before claiming a verified 9/10 game experience. Documentation maintenance note — 14 October 2026 The later Restart Game revision added six controller regressions, bringing the source suite to 99 tests; its source CI passed. The historical review scores above were not rerun for that small feature. See [requirements](REQUIREMENTS.md), [system context](ARCHITECTURE.md) and [issue tracker](ISSUES.md) for implemented versus queued behavior. Documentation content/HTML reviews are recorded separately in docs/REVIEW.md; their scores do not rate the game itself. P0/P1 source batch — 14 October 2026 F-025 canonical migration is published and verified at Pages baca95c7ba618d736730194b433254bf5058dd66, serving https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/. All 15 game files plus the legacy redirect matched intended hashes; 17 unrelated blobs remained unchanged. An independent blind medium review scored the bounded P0 migration 9/10. That score concerns deployment migration only. The migration published the preceding game revision; it does not establish that the new P1 source is live. F-001/F-002/F-005/F-009/F-010/F-016/F-018 now have source implementations and are Review. The new surface, world/goal, countdown/ledger, cutaway and QA tests support their source contracts. Final integrated totals, independent P1 findings and a separate P1 Pages release must be recorded after the integrated candidate is checked; none is inferred here. The old 93/99-test snapshots and 9.1/8.6 scores above remain historical. F-014 is Blocked because no physical phone is available. Browser capability discovery exposed no usable browser tools; the installed Sites instruction says “If $control-browser is unavailable, skip browser QA” (/root/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sites/1.0.0/skills/sites/SKILL.md). No browser screenshots, physical touch/gesture checks, real listening or GPU profiling are claimed. Geometry topology, material flags and camera-ray checks do not prove artifact-free pixels or transparent draw ordering. All P2 work remains Open. See [P0/P1 validation and release runbook](validation/P0-P1.md) for source checkpoints, build modes, exact manual scenarios and publication boundaries."},{"title":"Documentation reviews","url":"review.html","text":"Documentation review record Scope: requirements, system context and the generated HTML documentation. These scores rate documentation, not the game. Updated 14 October 2026. Blind review method Two fresh agents received the same committed candidate (bd227d3) in separate detached worktrees. They were instructed not to inspect Git history, sibling workspaces, other agents' findings or earlier documentation scores. They received the user’s documentation task without a target score. One reviewed content against source/tests; the other reviewed HTML fidelity, navigation, accessibility and behavior with DOM substitutes. Historical game scores in QUALITY.md were explicitly excluded as documentation evidence. Review First pass Confirmed findings Follow-up ------:------ Content 8.8/10 Root README still gave superseded publish instructions; helper validation/scope claims overstated; promised review record absent Resolved; follow-up 9.2/10 at 7899759 HTML format/content 8.6/10 Appearance toggle left diagram theme unchanged; failed legacy clipboard copy leaked a textarea Resolved; follow-up 9.0/10 at 7899759 The HTML critic’s first-pass categories were content/fidelity 9.1, format/navigation 8.5, accessibility 8.2 and functionality 8.6. Both first passes exceeded the user’s 8/10 threshold; confirmed issues were still repaired rather than ignored. Final follow-up results The original critics independently rechecked 7899759: content 9.2/10, HTML overall 9.0/10 (content/fidelity 9.1, format/navigation 8.7, accessibility 8.7, functionality 9.2). Both reported no remaining confirmed defect or blocker. This is follow-up validation after a blind first pass, not a new blind review. The final review-record edit only records these results. Both reran npm run docs:check: 11 pages, 614 local links/anchors, nine parsed diagrams and four passing DOM behavior tests. The HTML reviewer additionally held a diagram render open across rapid theme changes and confirmed no overlap with the correct final theme; repeated false clipboard returns, thrown exceptions and async Clipboard API rejection left no temporary textareas. Native browser limits below still apply. Evidence - Independent content review ran all 99 source tests, the documentation checks and all three runnable architecture/gameplay/world examples successfully. - Both critics independently ran the first candidate’s deterministic generator/link checks: 10 pages, 551 local links/anchors and nine diagrams. - The HTML critic parsed all nine diagram sources with the actual bundled Mermaid library; DOM substitutes exercised search, keyboard focus, clipboard and appearance persistence and reproduced both UI issues. - Revisions clarify that F-025 remains P0 and unimplemented; the preparation script checks only dist/index.html, replaces the old nested folder and also writes/stages .nojekyll at the Pages root. - HTML revisions serialize diagram theme rerenders and clean up temporary clipboard fields in a finally block. The source alternatives beneath diagrams remain readable. - The generator checks local document/source links and anchors, current generated output, offline runtime assets and traceability of all F-001–F-025 requirements. CI runs documentation checks before source tests/build. Verification limits No approved browser environment was available for real rendered screenshots, SVG layout, native dialogs, touchscreen interaction, actual file:// clipboard permission or print output. Source, parsed diagram syntax and DOM substitutes support the scores; they do not prove pixel-level visual quality. Manual browser smoke tests remain useful. The external 12ui design workflow was blocked by automatic approval review because sending the project brief to that service was not authorized. The documentation interface was built locally; no approval to disclose that brief is implied by these review scores. Maintenance Update this record when subsequent documentation changes receive new review evidence. Keep writer context and past documentation scores out of any new blind review packet. Rechecks by the original critics are follow-up validation, not a new blind first pass. Regenerate HTML after editing this record and keep requirements/issue statuses unchanged unless implementation evidence justifies a change."},{"title":"Project README","url":"project.html","text":"Flock: Little Robot, Big Pastures A cheerful 3D herding game for portrait phones. Guide a robotic sheepdog through handmade-looking countryside and bring your robotic flock home. Start with a small sheep flock, then tackle longer routes, larger groups, bridges, gates, and other destinations. Earn points for upgrades and autonomous helpers. Development branch: flock_3D_chatgpt-work. Documentation: [Start here](docs/README.md) · [HTML field guide](docs/html/index.html) · [Requirements](docs/REQUIREMENTS.md) · [Issue tracker](docs/ISSUES.md). Play the standalone development build at [novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/](https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/). The existing flock/dev/ game is preserved separately. Engine choice This build uses Three.js, with Vite for packaging. The requested ThreeRust engine at [threerust.com](https://threerust.com/) was investigated first. Its public offering is an early-access Rust/WebAssembly/WebGPU browser editor with RHAI scripting; a reusable runtime SDK suitable for this repository was not available through its public documentation. The user approved the Three.js fallback. This project does not claim to use ThreeRust or the separate three-rs/threers Rust projects. Three.js renders the world in true 3D with WebGL. The original characters and scenery use rounded clay-like shapes, colorful metal panels, visible mechanical joints, and glowing robot details. Run locally Use Node.js 22 or newer: sh npm ci npm run dev -- --host 0.0.0.0 Open the local URL printed by Vite. To run the simulation tests and build a standalone site: sh npm test npm run build npm run preview -- --host 0.0.0.0 The production build is written to dist/. Relative asset paths allow it to run beneath /Flock/dev2/ without a backend or CDN dependency. Playing - Use one thumb on the movement control to steer the robotic dog. - Drag anywhere on the meadow to steer, or use WASD/arrow keys on desktop. Double-tap the meadow or tap Woof for a short herding burst. - Approach the flock from behind to guide it toward the highlighted destination. - Keep the flock together while following routes through the countryside. - The first attended arrival starts a 30–60 second recovery countdown. Bring everyone home early or finish with a partial score at timeout. Any positive delivery opens the next meadow. - Earn bolts equal to improvements in your lifetime best proportional score, then spend them on skills and helpers. Replays at the same or a lower score pay no extra bolts. - Six skills have three tiers each: Turbo paws, Friendly signal, Scout drone, Buddy dog, Happy bark, and Flock harmony. The workshop shows the next tier's effect, cost, and level requirement. Skills appear throughout the first fifteen levels; the drone starts at level 10, and the more expensive Buddy dog starts at level 20. - Companions attend the largest connected herd. When Pip leaves, they hold that herd in place. Recover isolated stragglers yourself and return to lead the delivery. - Music starts after a player interaction, following mobile browser audio rules. Use the sound control to mute it. The opening pasture teaches herding with a small sheep flock and a nearby pen. Later procedural pastures introduce longer routes, rivers and bridges, roads and gates, more animals, and new destinations. Progress is stored locally in the browser; clearing site data clears that progress. Deployment The canonical development game is [https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/](https://novydhillon.github.io/Flock/dev2/), served from dev2/ on gh-pages. F-025 migration was published at Pages commit baca95c7ba618d736730194b433254bf5058dd66: all 15 game files and the legacy redirect were hash-verified; 17 unrelated blobs were unchanged. The previous /Flock/flock/dev2/ address redirects to the canonical game while retaining query and fragment. This migration deployed the preceding game revision; the new P1 source batch is awaiting its own release and verification. The source workflow tests the game, builds/checks release without QA, then builds/checks the QA-enabled development distribution for its artifact; upload does not publish Pages. npm run build makes a production bundle without QA. npm run build:dev makes the explicit development distribution with the QA menu. Use the intended build before preparing a release: sh npm run build:dev node scripts/check-build-mode.mjs development node scripts/deploy-pages.mjs /tmp/flock-pages-review git -C /tmp/flock-pages-review diff --cached --stat The helper validates local HTML/CSS/JavaScript asset references before creating a detached worktree from the locally fetched origin/gh-pages. It stages dev2/, the legacy redirect and .nojekyll only if missing; it never fetches, commits or pushes. Review the full diff, preserve unrelated content, and verify live hashes after native Pages publication. See [release instructions](docs/DEVELOPMENT.md) and the [P0/P1 validation runbook](docs/validation/P0-P1.md). Local development and explicit development builds expose QA · dev. Opening it starts an isolated test profile: QA 0 is a tutorial shortcut, public levels remain 1–100, and test edits never save to the normal profile. Jump levels, adjust test bolts, select an animal and edit its world coordinates while paused; invalid-placement override still respects meadow bounds. Reset starts a fresh test session, export downloads scenario JSON, and reload exits QA. Physical touch usability remains awaiting device validation. Scope This is a playable development build. It stores progress on-device and uses procedural geometry and generated material textures. It has no account system, online multiplayer, or cloud save service. The campaign contains 100 deterministic procedural meadows. Flock size increases to a maximum of 65; journey distance continues increasing afterward. Levels 1–5 have compact, single-route lessons. From level 6 the fenced roaming area expands, with one or two trails through level 15 and up to three afterward. Destinations rotate across compass directions, and winding trails connect real bridges and gate openings with clear staging space before the goal. Rivers continue beyond the perimeter; scenery hills stay outside playable ground. Sheep and cows use articulated, instanced leg poses at a stop-motion cadence. Grass and dirt have deterministic tileable color and bump textures, with decorative flowers, grasses and reeds. Puffy clouds drift with soft ground shadows. Later meadows cycle through sunshine, wind, light rain, and snow after level 8. Butterflies, birds and squirrels flee nearby robots; quiet bird and weather ambience shares the sound control. Automated checks cover simulation, upgrades, controller navigation, touch cancellation, denied storage, renderer matrices and resource disposal. The renderer tests use real Three.js geometry with a substituted GPU interface; they do not verify rendered pixels or phone frame rates. Actual mobile play, sound balance and GPU performance need device testing."}];
